var e=[{topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The body is built in layers: cells make tissues, tissues make organs, organs make systems. This level defines the parts of a cell, the four tissue types, and what homeostasis means.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Parts of a cell`},{kind:`para`,text:`Every cell has a plasma membrane (the outer boundary), cytoplasm (the jelly inside) and usually a nucleus (the control centre that holds DNA). Small structures in the cytoplasm that do defined jobs are called organelles. The membrane itself is a lipid bilayer with proteins floating in it, described by the fluid mosaic model of Singer and Nicolson.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Organelle`,`Main function`],rows:[[`Nucleus`,`Stores DNA, directs protein synthesis`],[`Nucleolus`,`Makes ribosomal RNA and ribosome subunits`],[`Rough ER`,`Ribosome-studded; synthesises export proteins`],[`Smooth ER`,`Lipid and steroid synthesis, drug detoxification`],[`Golgi apparatus`,`Modifies, packages and sorts proteins`],[`Mitochondrion`,`ATP production by oxidative phosphorylation`],[`Lysosome`,`Acid hydrolases; intracellular digestion`],[`Peroxisome`,`Beta-oxidation; catalase destroys H2O2`],[`Ribosome`,`Site of translation (protein synthesis)`],[`Centriole`,`Organises spindle fibres during cell division`]],caption:`The organelle-function pairs asked most often in GPAT.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Epithelial tissue: covers and lines surfaces, forms glands. Example: skin, gut lining.`,`Connective tissue: supports and binds. Example: bone, cartilage, blood, adipose.`,`Muscular tissue: contracts to produce movement. Example: biceps, heart, gut wall.`,`Nervous tissue: conducts electrical impulses. Example: brain, spinal cord, nerves.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Four tissue types`,text:`Every Cell Must Nourish: Epithelial, Connective, Muscular, Nervous.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Homeostasis means keeping the internal environment steady - temperature near 37 degrees C, blood pH 7.35 to 7.45, blood glucose about 70 to 110 mg/dL - even when the outside world changes. It works through feedback: a sensor detects change, a control centre compares it with a set point, and an effector corrects it.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Repeat one-liners`,text:`Mitochondrion is the powerhouse of the cell; lysosome is the suicide bag; ribosome is the protein factory; Golgi is the traffic police of the cell.`}]},{topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At exam level you are asked to classify transport processes by energy requirement and carrier, to name the epithelium of a given site, and to separate negative from positive feedback.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Membrane transport`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Process`,`Energy`,`Carrier`,`Example`],rows:[[`Simple diffusion`,`None`,`No`,`O2, CO2, urea, ethanol`],[`Facilitated diffusion`,`None`,`Yes`,`Glucose entry via GLUT`],[`Osmosis`,`None`,`Aquaporins`,`Water across membranes`],[`Primary active transport`,`ATP directly`,`Yes`,`Na+/K+ ATPase, H+/K+ ATPase`],[`Secondary active transport`,`Ion gradient`,`Yes`,`SGLT1 glucose in gut`],[`Endocytosis / exocytosis`,`ATP`,`Vesicle`,`LDL uptake, insulin release`]],caption:`Only primary active transport hydrolyses ATP at the carrier itself.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Na+/K+ ATPase: 3 Na+ pumped out + 2 K+ pumped in per 1 ATP`,caption:`It is electrogenic (net one positive charge leaves), which contributes about -4 mV to the resting membrane potential.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Epithelium by site`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type`,`Where found`,`Function`],rows:[[`Simple squamous`,`Alveoli, endothelium, Bowman capsule`,`Diffusion, filtration`],[`Simple cuboidal`,`Kidney tubules, thyroid follicles`,`Secretion, absorption`],[`Simple columnar`,`Stomach, intestine, gall bladder`,`Absorption, secretion`],[`Pseudostratified ciliated`,`Trachea, bronchi`,`Mucus propulsion`],[`Stratified squamous`,`Skin (keratinised), oesophagus`,`Protection from abrasion`],[`Transitional (urothelium)`,`Ureter, urinary bladder`,`Stretch without tearing`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Connective tissue proper: loose (areolar, adipose, reticular) and dense (regular in tendon, irregular in dermis).`,`Specialised connective tissue: cartilage (hyaline, elastic, fibrocartilage), bone, blood.`,`Main fibres: collagen (tensile strength), elastic (recoil), reticular (framework of organs).`,`Fibroblast makes matrix; mast cell releases histamine and heparin; macrophage phagocytoses.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Skeletal`,`Cardiac`,`Smooth`],rows:[[`Striations`,`Present`,`Present`,`Absent`],[`Nucleus`,`Multiple, peripheral`,`Single, central`,`Single, central`],[`Control`,`Voluntary (somatic)`,`Involuntary`,`Involuntary`],[`Intercalated discs`,`Absent`,`Present`,`Absent`],[`Ca2+ source`,`Sarcoplasmic reticulum`,`SR plus extracellular`,`Mostly extracellular`],[`Fatigue`,`Fatigues`,`Does not fatigue`,`Resistant, sustains tone`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Homeostasis and feedback`},{kind:`para`,text:`Negative feedback opposes the change and is the rule in physiology: rising blood glucose triggers insulin, which lowers glucose; rising body temperature triggers sweating and vasodilatation. Positive feedback amplifies the change and is used only when a rapid, self-terminating event is needed.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Positive feedback is not always harmful`,text:`Physiological positive feedback loops include oxytocin release during labour (Ferguson reflex), the platelet plug and clotting cascade, the LH surge before ovulation, and the Na+ influx phase of the action potential. Do not tick negative feedback for these.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`High yield`,text:`Aquaporin-2 in the collecting duct is the ADH-sensitive water channel; GLUT4 in muscle and adipose is the only insulin-dependent glucose transporter.`}]},{topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions turn on quantitative membrane physiology, junction types, and transporter families that drug classes act on.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`E(ion) = (61 / z) x log10 ([ion] outside / [ion] inside) mV, at 37 degrees C`,caption:`Nernst equation. E is the equilibrium potential, z the valency of the ion. E(K+) is about -90 mV, E(Na+) about +60 mV.`},{kind:`para`,text:`The resting membrane potential of a nerve cell is about -70 mV and sits close to E(K+) because the resting membrane is roughly 50 to 100 times more permeable to K+ than to Na+. The Goldman-Hodgkin-Katz equation weights each ion by its permeability and gives the true resting value.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Plasma osmolality (mOsm/kg) = 2 x [Na+] + [glucose] + [urea], all in mmol/L`,caption:`Normal 285 to 295 mOsm/kg. Sodium and its anions supply about 90 percent of plasma osmolality.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Junction`,`Protein`,`Role`],rows:[[`Tight junction (zonula occludens)`,`Claudin, occludin`,`Seals paracellular space`],[`Adherens junction (zonula adherens)`,`Cadherin`,`Anchors actin, holds cells together`],[`Desmosome (macula adherens)`,`Desmoglein`,`Spot weld to intermediate filaments`],[`Hemidesmosome`,`Integrin`,`Anchors cell to basement membrane`],[`Gap junction`,`Connexin`,`Ion and small solute flow between cells`]],caption:`Gap junctions give cardiac and smooth muscle their functional syncytium.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`GLUT1: RBC, blood-brain barrier, basal uptake.`,`GLUT2: liver, pancreatic beta cell, basolateral gut and kidney; low affinity, high capacity glucose sensor.`,`GLUT3: neurons, high affinity.`,`GLUT4: skeletal muscle and adipose; insulin recruits it to the membrane.`,`SGLT1: intestinal brush border; SGLT2: proximal tubule, target of dapagliflozin and empagliflozin.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`The cell cycle runs G1, S (DNA replication), G2 and M. Quiescent cells rest in G0. Labile cells (gut epithelium, bone marrow, skin) divide continuously; stable cells (hepatocyte, renal tubular cell) divide when stimulated; permanent cells (neuron, cardiac muscle, skeletal muscle) cannot divide, which is why myocardial infarction heals by scar and not by regeneration.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Pumps and their inhibitors`,text:`Na+/K+ ATPase is inhibited by digoxin and ouabain; H+/K+ ATPase of the gastric parietal cell by omeprazole; Na+-K+-2Cl- symporter of the thick ascending limb by furosemide; Na+-Cl- symporter of the distal tubule by thiazides.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Isotonic is not isosmotic`,text:`0.9 percent NaCl is both isosmotic and isotonic. A 1.8 percent urea solution is isosmotic with plasma but not isotonic, because urea crosses the membrane freely and causes haemolysis. Tonicity counts only the non-penetrating solutes.`}]},{topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Blood is a fluid connective tissue. An adult has about 5 litres, roughly 8 percent of body weight. This level covers what blood is made of, the normal counts you must memorise, and the basics of blood groups.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Plasma is about 55 percent of blood volume; formed elements are about 45 percent.`,`Plasma is 91 to 92 percent water and 7 to 8 percent proteins (albumin, globulins, fibrinogen).`,`Formed elements are erythrocytes (RBC), leucocytes (WBC) and thrombocytes (platelets).`,`Serum is plasma minus fibrinogen and the other clotting factors, so serum does not clot.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Parameter`,`Normal adult value`],rows:[[`RBC count, male`,`4.5 to 6.0 million per cu mm`],[`RBC count, female`,`4.0 to 5.5 million per cu mm`],[`Total WBC count`,`4000 to 11000 per cu mm`],[`Platelet count`,`1.5 to 4.0 lakh per cu mm (150 to 400 x 10^9/L)`],[`Haemoglobin, male`,`13 to 18 g/dL`],[`Haemoglobin, female`,`12 to 16 g/dL`],[`Haematocrit (PCV), male`,`40 to 54 percent`],[`Haematocrit (PCV), female`,`36 to 47 percent`],[`ESR, Westergren, male`,`0 to 15 mm in first hour`],[`ESR, Westergren, female`,`0 to 20 mm in first hour`],[`RBC lifespan`,`120 days`],[`Platelet lifespan`,`8 to 10 days`]],caption:`These exact numbers are asked directly. Learn them with units.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Neutrophils 40 to 75 percent - first responders to bacteria.`,`Lymphocytes 20 to 45 percent - immunity, T and B cells.`,`Monocytes 2 to 10 percent - become tissue macrophages.`,`Eosinophils 1 to 6 percent - parasites and allergy.`,`Basophils 0 to 1 percent - release histamine and heparin.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`WBC differential in decreasing order`,text:`Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas: Neutrophil > Lymphocyte > Monocyte > Eosinophil > Basophil.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Blood transports oxygen, carbon dioxide, nutrients, hormones and waste; it defends the body through white cells and antibodies; it stops bleeding by clotting; and it helps regulate body temperature, pH and fluid balance.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Donor rules`,text:`O negative is the universal donor of packed red cells; AB positive is the universal recipient. For plasma the rule reverses - AB is the universal plasma donor.`}]},{topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The examinable core here is haemopoiesis, the ABO and Rh systems, and the coagulation cascade split into intrinsic, extrinsic and common pathways.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Haemopoiesis`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Mesoblastic stage: yolk sac, first 2 months of intrauterine life.`,`Hepatic stage: liver and spleen, months 2 to 7.`,`Myeloid stage: bone marrow, from the 7th month onward and for life.`,`In adults red marrow persists in flat bones - sternum, ribs, vertebrae, ilium - and the ends of long bones.`,`Erythropoietin from peritubular interstitial cells of the kidney is the main stimulus; hypoxia is the trigger.`]},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Proerythroblast (megaloblast)`,`Early or basophilic normoblast`,`Intermediate or polychromatic normoblast`,`Late or orthochromatic normoblast - nucleus extruded here`,`Reticulocyte - still has ribosomal RNA, normally 0.5 to 1.5 percent of RBC`,`Mature erythrocyte - biconcave, no nucleus, no mitochondria`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Blood groups`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Group`,`Antigen on RBC`,`Antibody in plasma`,`Can receive from`],rows:[[`A`,`A`,`Anti-B`,`A, O`],[`B`,`B`,`Anti-A`,`B, O`],[`AB`,`A and B`,`None`,`A, B, AB, O`],[`O`,`None`,`Anti-A and anti-B`,`O only`]],caption:`ABO antibodies are natural IgM; Rh antibodies are immune IgG formed only after exposure.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Rh grouping depends on the D antigen. An Rh negative mother carrying an Rh positive fetus can be sensitised at delivery; in a later Rh positive pregnancy her IgG anti-D crosses the placenta and causes haemolytic disease of the newborn, also called erythroblastosis fetalis. Anti-D immunoglobulin given to the mother within 72 hours of delivery prevents sensitisation.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Coagulation cascade`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Intrinsic pathway: contact of factor XII with collagen or glass activates XII to XIIa, then XI to XIa, then IX to IXa; IXa with VIIIa, Ca2+ and platelet phospholipid activates factor X. Slower, minutes.`,`Extrinsic pathway: tissue injury releases tissue factor (factor III), which with factor VIIa and Ca2+ activates factor X directly. Fast, about 15 seconds.`,`Common pathway: Xa with Va, Ca2+ and phospholipid forms prothrombinase, which converts prothrombin (II) to thrombin (IIa); thrombin converts fibrinogen (I) to fibrin monomer; factor XIIIa cross-links it into a stable clot.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Test`,`Pathway tested`,`Normal value`],rows:[[`Bleeding time`,`Platelet and vessel function`,`2 to 7 minutes (Ivy)`],[`Clotting time`,`Overall coagulation`,`5 to 11 minutes`],[`Prothrombin time (PT/INR)`,`Extrinsic plus common`,`11 to 16 seconds`],[`aPTT`,`Intrinsic plus common`,`25 to 40 seconds`]],caption:`Warfarin is monitored by PT/INR; unfractionated heparin by aPTT.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`In vivo anticoagulants: heparin (activates antithrombin III), warfarin (vitamin K epoxide reductase inhibitor), direct oral agents such as dabigatran and rivaroxaban.`,`In vitro anticoagulants: EDTA (chelates Ca2+, best for cell counts), sodium citrate 3.2 percent (coagulation studies and blood banking), oxalate, heparin (blood gas samples).`,`Fibrinolysis: plasminogen is converted to plasmin by tissue plasminogen activator, urokinase or streptokinase; plasmin digests fibrin into fibrin degradation products including D-dimer.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Calcium is factor IV`,text:`Calcium is needed at almost every step of both pathways, which is why chelating it with EDTA, citrate or oxalate prevents clotting in a test tube. There is no factor VI - the name was withdrawn.`}]},{topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`This level fixes the factor names, the vitamin K dependent group, the red cell indices you may be asked to calculate, and lymphoid organ detail.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Factor`,`Common name`],rows:[[`I`,`Fibrinogen`],[`II`,`Prothrombin`],[`III`,`Tissue factor / thromboplastin`],[`IV`,`Calcium`],[`V`,`Labile factor / proaccelerin`],[`VII`,`Stable factor / proconvertin`],[`VIII`,`Antihaemophilic factor A`],[`IX`,`Christmas factor`],[`X`,`Stuart-Prower factor`],[`XI`,`Plasma thromboplastin antecedent`],[`XII`,`Hageman factor / contact factor`],[`XIII`,`Fibrin stabilising factor`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Vitamin K dependent factors`,text:`Think 1972: factors II, VII, IX and X, plus the natural anticoagulants protein C and protein S. All need gamma-carboxylation of glutamate residues, the step warfarin blocks.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`MCV (fL) = (Haematocrit percent x 10) / RBC count in millions per cu mm`,caption:`Mean corpuscular volume. Normal 80 to 100 fL.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`MCH (pg) = (Hb in g/dL x 10) / RBC in millions per cu mm ; MCHC (percent) = (Hb x 100) / Haematocrit`,caption:`MCH normal 27 to 32 pg; MCHC normal 32 to 36 percent (g/dL). Low MCV plus low MCHC means microcytic hypochromic anaemia, classically iron deficiency.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Haemophilia A: deficiency of factor VIII, X-linked recessive, prolonged aPTT with normal PT and normal bleeding time.`,`Haemophilia B (Christmas disease): factor IX deficiency, same laboratory picture.`,`von Willebrand disease: autosomal, prolonged bleeding time plus prolonged aPTT because vWF carries factor VIII.`,`Vitamin K deficiency or warfarin prolongs PT first, because factor VII has the shortest half-life (about 6 hours).`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Agent`,`Mechanism`,`Chief use`],rows:[[`Heparin`,`Activates antithrombin III`,`In vivo and in vitro, acts immediately`],[`Warfarin`,`Blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase`,`In vivo only, onset 2 to 3 days`],[`EDTA`,`Chelates calcium`,`Haematology cell counts`],[`Sodium citrate`,`Chelates calcium, reversible`,`Blood bank, PT and aPTT samples`],[`Potassium oxalate`,`Precipitates calcium`,`Glucose estimation with fluoride`]]},{kind:`para`,text:`The lymphatic system returns about 2 to 4 litres of interstitial fluid to the circulation each day. Lymph from the whole body except the right upper quadrant drains into the thoracic duct, which empties into the junction of the left subclavian and left internal jugular veins; the right lymphatic duct drains the right head, neck, arm and thorax. Lymph from the intestine is milky after a fatty meal and is called chyle because it carries chylomicrons.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Spleen versus lymph node`,text:`The spleen has efferent lymphatics but no afferent lymphatics, so it filters blood and not lymph. It is the graveyard of red cells, performs culling and pitting, and in the fetus it is a site of haemopoiesis. Thymus is the primary lymphoid organ for T cells; bone marrow for B cells.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`ESR movers`,text:`ESR rises in pregnancy, tuberculosis, rheumatoid arthritis, multiple myeloma and any acute phase response, mainly because fibrinogen and globulins promote rouleaux. It falls in polycythaemia, sickle cell disease and congestive cardiac failure.`}]},{topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The heart is a four-chambered muscular pump about the size of a closed fist, lying in the mediastinum with two thirds of it to the left of the midline. It pumps roughly 5 litres of blood every minute.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Chambers and valves`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Right atrium receives deoxygenated blood from the superior and inferior vena cava and the coronary sinus.`,`Right ventricle pumps it through the pulmonary trunk to the lungs.`,`Left atrium receives oxygenated blood from four pulmonary veins.`,`Left ventricle pumps it into the aorta; its wall is the thickest, about 3 times the right ventricle.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Valve`,`Position`,`Cusps`],rows:[[`Tricuspid`,`Right atrium to right ventricle`,`3`],[`Mitral (bicuspid)`,`Left atrium to left ventricle`,`2`],[`Pulmonary`,`Right ventricle to pulmonary trunk`,`3 semilunar`],[`Aortic`,`Left ventricle to aorta`,`3 semilunar`]],caption:`Valves are passive - they open and close on pressure differences alone.`},{kind:`para`,text:`The heart wall has three layers: endocardium (inner lining), myocardium (cardiac muscle, the thickest) and epicardium. Around it is the pericardium, a double-walled sac holding 15 to 50 mL of pericardial fluid that reduces friction.`},{kind:`para`,text:`The first heart sound, lub, is produced by closure of the atrioventricular valves at the start of ventricular systole. The second sound, dub, is produced by closure of the aortic and pulmonary valves at the start of diastole.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Valve cusps`,text:`LAB RAT: Left Atrium Bicuspid, Right Atrium Tricuspid.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Blood supply of the heart`,text:`The left coronary artery divides into the left anterior descending and circumflex branches; the LAD supplies the anterior interventricular septum and is the artery most often occluded in myocardial infarction. Venous blood returns through the coronary sinus into the right atrium.`}]},{topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Cardiac cycle timings, the conducting system, ECG waves and cardiac output arithmetic are the four blocks that carry almost every GPAT cardiovascular question.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Conducting system`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Structure`,`Intrinsic rate`,`Note`],rows:[[`SA node`,`70 to 80 per minute`,`Pacemaker; in right atrium near SVC opening`],[`AV node`,`40 to 60 per minute`,`Only electrical link atria to ventricles; delay 0.09 s`],[`Bundle of His and branches`,`40 to 45 per minute`,`Conducts through interventricular septum`],[`Purkinje fibres`,`25 to 40 per minute`,`Fastest conduction, about 4 m/s`]],caption:`Conduction is slowest at the AV node (about 0.05 m/s) so the atria empty before the ventricles contract.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Cardiac cycle`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Phase`,`Duration at 75 bpm`,`Valves`],rows:[[`Atrial systole`,`0.1 s`,`AV open, semilunar closed`],[`Isovolumetric contraction`,`0.05 s`,`All four closed`],[`Ventricular ejection`,`0.3 s`,`Semilunar open`],[`Isovolumetric relaxation`,`0.08 s`,`All four closed`],[`Ventricular filling`,`0.4 s approx`,`AV open`]],caption:`Total cycle 0.8 s: atrial systole 0.1 s, ventricular systole 0.3 s, diastole 0.5 s (atrial diastole 0.7 s).`},{kind:`heading`,text:`ECG`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Wave or interval`,`Represents`,`Normal duration`],rows:[[`P wave`,`Atrial depolarisation`,`0.1 s`],[`PR interval`,`Atrial depolarisation plus AV nodal delay`,`0.12 to 0.20 s`],[`QRS complex`,`Ventricular depolarisation`,`0.08 to 0.10 s`],[`ST segment`,`Ventricles fully depolarised`,`0.08 s`],[`T wave`,`Ventricular repolarisation`,`0.2 s`],[`QT interval`,`Total ventricular systole`,`0.4 s approx`]],caption:`Atrial repolarisation is hidden inside the QRS complex, which is why there is no separate wave for it.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Cardiac output (CO) = Stroke volume (SV) x Heart rate (HR)`,caption:`Normal CO about 5 L/min = 70 mL x 72 per minute.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`SV = End diastolic volume - End systolic volume ; Ejection fraction = SV / EDV x 100`,caption:`EDV about 130 mL, ESV about 60 mL, so SV about 70 mL and ejection fraction about 55 to 70 percent.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Mean arterial pressure (MAP) = Diastolic BP + (Pulse pressure / 3) ; Pulse pressure = Systolic - Diastolic`,caption:`For 120/80 mmHg, pulse pressure is 40 mmHg and MAP is about 93 mmHg.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Systemic circulation: left ventricle to aorta to tissues to vena cava to right atrium; high pressure.`,`Pulmonary circulation: right ventricle to lungs to left atrium; low pressure, about 25/8 mmHg.`,`Portal circulation: gut capillaries to portal vein to liver sinusoids - two capillary beds in series.`,`Coronary circulation: left ventricular myocardium is perfused mainly during diastole.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Baroreceptor reflex`,text:`Baroreceptors in the carotid sinus (glossopharyngeal nerve, IX) and aortic arch (vagus, X) sense a rise in BP, increase their firing, and the vasomotor centre responds with vagal stimulation and sympathetic withdrawal - so heart rate, contractility and peripheral resistance all fall and BP returns to normal. It is a fast, seconds-to-minutes negative feedback loop.`}]},{topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced cardiovascular questions test the cardiac action potential, Starling relationships and the quantitative side of pressure and flow.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Phase`,`Event`,`Ion movement`],rows:[[`0`,`Rapid depolarisation`,`Fast Na+ influx`],[`1`,`Initial repolarisation`,`K+ efflux, Na+ channels close`],[`2`,`Plateau (unique to cardiac)`,`Ca2+ influx via L-type channels balances K+ efflux`],[`3`,`Repolarisation`,`K+ efflux`],[`4`,`Resting potential`,`Na+/K+ ATPase restores gradients`]],caption:`The plateau makes the ventricular action potential last about 250 to 300 ms and gives a long refractory period, so cardiac muscle cannot be tetanised.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Pacemaker cells of the SA node have no stable resting potential. Their phase 4 slow depolarisation is carried by the funny current If (Na+ influx) and T-type Ca2+ channels; phase 0 is due to L-type Ca2+ influx, not Na+. Ivabradine slows the heart by blocking If selectively.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Cardiac index = Cardiac output / Body surface area`,caption:`Normal 2.5 to 4.0 L/min/m2. Used to compare output between people of different size.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`CO (Fick) = O2 consumption / (Arterial O2 content - Venous O2 content)`,caption:`Example: 250 mL/min divided by (0.20 - 0.15) mL O2 per mL blood = 5000 mL/min = 5 L/min.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`MAP = Cardiac output x Total peripheral resistance ; Resistance is proportional to 1 / r^4 (Poiseuille)`,caption:`Halving the radius of an arteriole raises its resistance 16-fold, which is why arterioles are the main resistance vessels.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Frank-Starling law: within limits, the greater the end diastolic fibre length (preload), the greater the force of contraction.`,`Bainbridge reflex: a rise in right atrial pressure or venous return increases heart rate.`,`Marey law: heart rate is inversely proportional to arterial blood pressure.`,`Long term BP control is renal and hormonal - the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and ADH, not baroreceptors, which reset within days.`,`Veins hold about 64 percent of the blood volume and act as capacitance vessels.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Where does each ECG deflection actually come from`,text:`The T wave is ventricular repolarisation, not relaxation, and it is upright even though repolarisation is opposite in direction, because repolarisation spreads from epicardium to endocardium. A prolonged PR interval means first degree heart block; a widened QRS means bundle branch block.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Numbers worth memorising`,text:`Normal central venous pressure 0 to 8 mmHg, pulmonary capillary wedge pressure 6 to 12 mmHg, left ventricular systolic pressure 120 mmHg, right ventricular systolic pressure 25 mmHg, aortic diastolic pressure 80 mmHg.`}]},{topicId:`ap-respiratory`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Respiration brings oxygen to the blood and removes carbon dioxide. The tract is divided into a conducting zone that only carries air and a respiratory zone where gas exchange happens.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Conducting zone: nose, pharynx, larynx, trachea, bronchi, bronchioles down to terminal bronchioles. This is the anatomical dead space, about 150 mL.`,`Respiratory zone: respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, alveolar sacs and alveoli - about 300 million alveoli with a surface area near 70 square metres.`,`The trachea divides at the level of T4 or T5 into right and left main bronchi.`,`The right bronchus is shorter, wider and more vertical, so inhaled foreign bodies usually go right.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`The alveolar wall is lined by type I pneumocytes (thin, for gas exchange) and type II pneumocytes, which secrete surfactant. Surfactant is mainly dipalmitoyl phosphatidylcholine; it lowers surface tension and stops the alveoli collapsing. A premature baby lacking surfactant develops respiratory distress syndrome.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Quiet inspiration is active: the diaphragm (phrenic nerve, C3, C4 and C5) contracts and flattens, the external intercostals lift the ribs, thoracic volume rises, intrapulmonary pressure falls below atmospheric and air flows in. Quiet expiration is passive - the elastic recoil of the lung does the work.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Phrenic nerve`,text:`C3, 4, 5 keep the diaphragm alive.`},{kind:`para`,text:`Normal breathing rate in an adult is 12 to 16 breaths per minute. Tidal volume is about 500 mL, so pulmonary ventilation is roughly 6 litres per minute.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Pleural pressure is negative`,text:`Intrapleural pressure stays subatmospheric throughout the cycle - about -5 cm H2O at end expiration and -8 cm H2O at end inspiration. If air enters the pleural space (pneumothorax) the pressure becomes atmospheric and the lung collapses.`}]},{topicId:`ap-respiratory`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Lung volumes and capacities, and the arithmetic linking them, are the single most examined part of respiratory physiology. A capacity is always the sum of two or more volumes.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Volume or capacity`,`Definition`,`Normal adult value`],rows:[[`Tidal volume (TV)`,`Air in or out in quiet breathing`,`500 mL`],[`Inspiratory reserve volume (IRV)`,`Extra air inhaled forcibly`,`3000 to 3300 mL`],[`Expiratory reserve volume (ERV)`,`Extra air exhaled forcibly`,`1100 to 1200 mL`],[`Residual volume (RV)`,`Air left after maximal expiration`,`1200 mL`],[`Inspiratory capacity (IC)`,`TV + IRV`,`3500 mL`],[`Functional residual capacity (FRC)`,`ERV + RV`,`2300 to 2400 mL`],[`Vital capacity (VC)`,`IRV + TV + ERV`,`4600 to 4800 mL`],[`Total lung capacity (TLC)`,`VC + RV`,`5800 to 6000 mL`]],caption:`Values are for a young adult male; subtract roughly 20 to 25 percent for a female.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`IC = TV + IRV ; FRC = ERV + RV ; VC = IRV + TV + ERV ; TLC = VC + RV = IRV + TV + ERV + RV`,caption:`Learn these four relations - most numerical questions are one substitution away.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Alveolar ventilation = (Tidal volume - Dead space) x Respiratory rate`,caption:`Example: (500 - 150) x 12 = 4200 mL/min. Deep slow breathing gives better alveolar ventilation than rapid shallow breathing at the same minute volume.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Gas exchange and transport`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Site`,`pO2 (mmHg)`,`pCO2 (mmHg)`],rows:[[`Atmospheric air`,`159`,`0.3`],[`Alveolar air`,`104`,`40`],[`Arterial blood`,`95 to 100`,`40`],[`Venous blood`,`40`,`45`],[`Tissue`,`40 or less`,`45 or more`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Oxygen transport: 97 percent bound to haemoglobin as oxyhaemoglobin, 3 percent dissolved in plasma. 1 g of Hb carries 1.34 mL of O2.`,`Carbon dioxide transport: about 70 percent as bicarbonate, 23 percent as carbamino compounds, 7 percent dissolved.`,`Carbonic anhydrase inside the red cell drives CO2 + H2O to H2CO3 to H+ + HCO3-; the chloride shift (Hamburger phenomenon) exchanges HCO3- for Cl-.`,`CO2 diffuses about 20 times faster than O2 because it is far more soluble, so hypoxia appears before hypercapnia in diffusion defects.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Oxygen dissociation curve`},{kind:`para`,text:`The curve is sigmoid because of cooperative binding: P50, the pO2 at which haemoglobin is 50 percent saturated, is 26 to 27 mmHg. A right shift means reduced affinity and easier unloading to tissues; a left shift means tighter binding.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Shift`,`Caused by`,`Effect`],rows:[[`Right shift`,`Increased CO2, H+ (low pH), temperature, 2,3-DPG`,`O2 released more easily, P50 rises`],[`Left shift`,`Decreased CO2, alkalosis, low temperature, low 2,3-DPG`,`O2 held tightly, P50 falls`],[`Left shift also`,`Fetal Hb, carbon monoxide, methaemoglobin`,`Poor tissue delivery`]],caption:`The Bohr effect is the CO2 and H+ driven right shift in tissues; the Haldane effect is deoxygenated Hb carrying more CO2.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Right shift`,text:`CADET, face Right: CO2, Acid, 2,3-DPG, Exercise, Temperature all shift the curve to the right.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Regulation of respiration`,text:`The central chemoreceptors in the medulla respond to CSF H+ generated from CO2 and are the main minute-to-minute drive. Peripheral chemoreceptors in the carotid and aortic bodies respond to a fall in pO2 below about 60 mmHg. In chronic CO2 retention the hypoxic drive takes over, which is why uncontrolled oxygen is dangerous in severe COPD.`}]},{topicId:`ap-respiratory`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At the highest level you are asked about compliance, ventilation-perfusion matching, respiratory centres and the physics of the alveolus.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Compliance = Change in volume / Change in pressure`,caption:`Normal lung compliance about 200 mL per cm H2O. It falls in fibrosis and in surfactant deficiency, and rises in emphysema where elastic tissue is destroyed.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Laplace law for an alveolus: P = 2T / r`,caption:`P is collapsing pressure, T surface tension, r radius. A small alveolus would collapse into a large one; surfactant prevents this by lowering T more in small alveoli.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Normal ventilation-perfusion ratio for the whole lung is about 0.8 (4.2 L/min alveolar ventilation over 5 L/min perfusion).`,`At the apex of an upright lung V/Q is high (about 3.3) - more ventilation than perfusion; at the base V/Q is low (about 0.6).`,`Shunt is perfusion without ventilation (V/Q = 0), as in atelectasis; dead space is ventilation without perfusion (V/Q = infinity), as in pulmonary embolism.`,`Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction diverts blood away from poorly ventilated alveoli - the opposite of the systemic circulation response.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Centre`,`Location`,`Function`],rows:[[`Dorsal respiratory group`,`Medulla`,`Basic inspiratory rhythm`],[`Ventral respiratory group`,`Medulla`,`Forced inspiration and expiration`],[`Pneumotaxic centre`,`Upper pons`,`Limits inspiration, raises rate`],[`Apneustic centre`,`Lower pons`,`Prolongs inspiration`],[`Pre-Botzinger complex`,`Medulla`,`Rhythm generator`]]},{kind:`para`,text:`The Hering-Breuer inflation reflex is carried by vagal stretch receptors: when tidal volume exceeds about 1.5 litres they fire and inhibit inspiration, protecting against overinflation. It is not active in quiet breathing in adults.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`FEV1/FVC ratio is normally more than 80 percent. It falls in obstructive disease (asthma, COPD) and is preserved or raised in restrictive disease, where both FEV1 and FVC fall together.`,`Hypoxic hypoxia: low arterial pO2, as at high altitude. Anaemic hypoxia: normal pO2, low O2 carrying capacity. Stagnant hypoxia: poor blood flow. Histotoxic hypoxia: cyanide blocks cytochrome oxidase so tissue cannot use O2.`,`Cyanosis needs at least 5 g/dL of reduced haemoglobin, so a severely anaemic patient may be hypoxic without ever turning blue.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Residual volume cannot be measured by spirometry`,text:`Because RV never leaves the lung, spirometry cannot measure RV, FRC or TLC. These need helium dilution, nitrogen washout or body plethysmography.`}]},{topicId:`ap-nervous`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The nervous system is the fast control system of the body. It works by sending electrical signals along cells called neurons, and it is divided into the central nervous system (brain and spinal cord) and the peripheral nervous system (all the nerves outside it).`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The neuron`},{kind:`para`,text:`A neuron has a cell body (soma) containing the nucleus, many short branching dendrites that receive signals, and one long axon that carries the signal away. Many axons are wrapped in myelin, a fatty insulating sheath. Myelin is made by Schwann cells outside the brain and by oligodendrocytes inside it. The small gaps between myelin segments are the nodes of Ranvier.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Dendrite: receives incoming signals and carries them towards the cell body.`,`Axon: carries the outgoing signal away from the cell body, sometimes over a metre.`,`Synapse: the tiny gap where one neuron passes a message to the next using a chemical.`,`Neurotransmitter: the chemical messenger released at the synapse, for example acetylcholine.`,`Neuroglia: supporting cells (astrocytes, oligodendrocytes, microglia, ependymal cells) that do not conduct impulses.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`How the signal travels`},{kind:`para`,text:`A resting neuron is negative inside, about -70 millivolts. When it is stimulated strongly enough, sodium rushes in and the inside briefly becomes positive. This flip is the nerve impulse or action potential. Potassium then leaves and the cell returns to rest. In a myelinated axon the impulse jumps from node to node, which is much faster - this is called saltatory conduction.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Parts of the brain`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Part`,`Main job`],rows:[[`Cerebrum`,`Thinking, memory, voluntary movement, sensation`],[`Cerebellum`,`Balance, posture, coordination of movement`],[`Thalamus`,`Relay station for all sensations except smell`],[`Hypothalamus`,`Temperature, hunger, thirst, hormones, emotion`],[`Medulla oblongata`,`Heart rate, breathing, blood pressure, vomiting`],[`Pons`,`Bridge between cerebrum and cerebellum; breathing`],[`Midbrain`,`Visual and auditory reflexes, eye movement`]]},{kind:`para`,text:`The spinal cord runs inside the vertebral column and carries messages up to and down from the brain. It also handles reflexes on its own: touching something hot makes you pull your hand away before the brain even knows about it.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Brain and meninges`,text:`The three coverings of the brain from outside in are Dura, Arachnoid, Pia - remember DAP. Cerebrospinal fluid flows between arachnoid and pia, in the subarachnoid space.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Straight recall facts`,text:`Resting membrane potential -70 mV; threshold about -55 mV; there are 12 pairs of cranial nerves and 31 pairs of spinal nerves; CSF volume is about 150 mL and is produced by the choroid plexus.`}]},{topicId:`ap-nervous`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At the exam level you must be able to link each phase of the action potential to the ion channel that causes it, compare the two limbs of the autonomic nervous system, and place each cranial nerve and each special sense receptor.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Action potential, phase by phase`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Phase`,`Ion movement`,`Result`],rows:[[`Resting state`,`Na+/K+ ATPase 3 Na+ out, 2 K+ in`,`Inside -70 mV`],[`Depolarisation`,`Voltage-gated Na+ channels open`,`Rises to +30 mV`],[`Repolarisation`,`Na+ channels inactivate, K+ channels open`,`Returns to negative`],[`Hyperpolarisation`,`K+ efflux overshoots`,`Reaches about -90 mV`],[`Restoration`,`Na+/K+ pump re-establishes gradients`,`Back to -70 mV`]],caption:`Only depolarisation is sodium-driven; every later phase is potassium-driven.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Nernst potential E(ion) = 61 / z x log10 ([ion]outside / [ion]inside)`,caption:`E in millivolts at 37 degrees C, z the valency. Equilibrium potential is about +60 mV for Na+ and -90 mV for K+; the resting potential lies close to E(K+) because the membrane is far more permeable to potassium.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Absolute refractory period: Na+ channels are inactivated, so no stimulus of any size can fire a second impulse. This sets the maximum firing frequency and prevents backward conduction.`,`Relative refractory period: a stronger than normal stimulus can fire the neuron because some Na+ channels have recovered.`,`The action potential obeys the all-or-none law: a stronger stimulus does not make a bigger spike, it makes more spikes per second.`,`Conduction velocity rises with axon diameter and with myelination. Type A alpha fibres conduct at 70 to 120 m/s; unmyelinated C fibres at only 0.5 to 2 m/s.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Synaptic transmission`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`The action potential reaches the presynaptic terminal and depolarises it.`,`Voltage-gated calcium channels open and Ca2+ enters the terminal.`,`Ca2+ triggers fusion of synaptic vesicles with the membrane (SNARE proteins) and exocytosis of transmitter.`,`Transmitter diffuses across the 20 to 40 nm cleft and binds postsynaptic receptors.`,`An excitatory transmitter opens Na+ channels giving an EPSP; an inhibitory one opens Cl- or K+ channels giving an IPSP.`,`Transmitter action ends by enzymatic breakdown, reuptake or diffusion.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Transmitter`,`Main receptors`,`Note`],rows:[[`Acetylcholine`,`Nicotinic (ion channel), muscarinic (GPCR)`,`Broken down by acetylcholinesterase`],[`Noradrenaline`,`Alpha 1, alpha 2, beta 1, beta 2`,`Removed mainly by reuptake 1`],[`Dopamine`,`D1 to D5`,`Deficient in Parkinson disease`],[`Serotonin (5-HT)`,`5-HT1 to 5-HT7`,`Mood, sleep, vomiting`],[`GABA`,`GABA-A (Cl- channel), GABA-B (GPCR)`,`Chief inhibitory transmitter of brain`],[`Glycine`,`Glycine receptor (Cl- channel)`,`Chief inhibitory transmitter of cord`],[`Glutamate`,`NMDA, AMPA, kainate`,`Chief excitatory transmitter of CNS`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Reflex arc and cranial nerves`},{kind:`para`,text:`A reflex arc has five parts: receptor, afferent (sensory) neuron, centre in the CNS, efferent (motor) neuron, effector. The knee jerk is monosynaptic - sensory neuron synapses directly on the motor neuron - while the withdrawal reflex is polysynaptic and includes interneurons.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Nerve`,`Name`,`Type and function`],rows:[[`I`,`Olfactory`,`Sensory - smell`],[`II`,`Optic`,`Sensory - vision`],[`III`,`Oculomotor`,`Motor - most eye muscles, pupil constriction`],[`IV`,`Trochlear`,`Motor - superior oblique`],[`V`,`Trigeminal`,`Mixed - face sensation, mastication`],[`VI`,`Abducens`,`Motor - lateral rectus`],[`VII`,`Facial`,`Mixed - facial expression, taste anterior 2/3`],[`VIII`,`Vestibulocochlear`,`Sensory - hearing and balance`],[`IX`,`Glossopharyngeal`,`Mixed - taste posterior 1/3, swallowing`],[`X`,`Vagus`,`Mixed - parasympathetic to thorax and abdomen`],[`XI`,`Accessory`,`Motor - sternocleidomastoid, trapezius`],[`XII`,`Hypoglossal`,`Motor - tongue muscles`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Cranial nerves`,text:`Names: Oh Oh Oh To Touch And Feel Very Green Vegetables AH. Type: Some Say Marry Money But My Brother Says Big Brains Matter More (S sensory, M motor, B both).`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Autonomic nervous system`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Sympathetic`,`Parasympathetic`],rows:[[`Outflow`,`Thoracolumbar T1-L2`,`Craniosacral III, VII, IX, X, S2-S4`],[`Ganglia`,`Near cord (paravertebral chain)`,`Near or in the organ`],[`Preganglionic fibre`,`Short`,`Long`],[`Postganglionic transmitter`,`Noradrenaline (sweat glands ACh)`,`Acetylcholine`],[`Heart`,`Rate and force increase (beta 1)`,`Rate falls (M2)`],[`Bronchi`,`Dilate (beta 2)`,`Constrict, secretions rise (M3)`],[`Pupil`,`Dilates - mydriasis (alpha 1)`,`Constricts - miosis (M3)`],[`Gut motility`,`Reduced, sphincters contract`,`Increased, sphincters relax`],[`Overall role`,`Fight or flight`,`Rest and digest`]],caption:`All preganglionic fibres of both limbs release acetylcholine onto nicotinic receptors.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Special senses`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Eye: three coats - fibrous (sclera and cornea), vascular (choroid, ciliary body, iris) and nervous (retina). Rods handle dim light and contain rhodopsin; cones handle colour and detail and are packed in the fovea centralis.`,`Visual pathway: retina, optic nerve, optic chiasma (nasal fibres cross), optic tract, lateral geniculate body, optic radiation, occipital visual cortex (area 17).`,`Ear: external ear collects sound, middle ear ossicles (malleus, incus, stapes) amplify about 20-fold, inner ear cochlea converts vibration to nerve impulses in the organ of Corti.`,`Hearing pathway: organ of Corti, cochlear nerve, cochlear nuclei, superior olive, inferior colliculus, medial geniculate body, temporal auditory cortex (areas 41 and 42).`,`Balance depends on the semicircular canals (angular acceleration) and the utricle and saccule (linear acceleration and head position).`,`Taste has five primary modalities - sweet, sour, salt, bitter, umami - carried by cranial nerves VII, IX and X. Smell is the only sensation that reaches the cortex without relaying in the thalamus.`]}]},{topicId:`ap-nervous`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced questions probe the quantitative side of excitability, named tracts and their lesions, receptor subtypes, and the biochemistry of the visual cycle.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Goldman-Hodgkin-Katz: Vm = 61 x log10 ( (PK[K]o + PNa[Na]o + PCl[Cl]i) / (PK[K]i + PNa[Na]i + PCl[Cl]o) )`,caption:`P is permeability. At rest PK is roughly 100 times PNa, so Vm sits near E(K). At the peak of the spike PNa exceeds PK by about 20 times, so Vm swings towards E(Na).`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Tetrodotoxin blocks voltage-gated Na+ channels from outside; local anaesthetics block the inactivated state from inside; tetraethylammonium blocks K+ channels.`,`The Na+/K+ ATPase is electrogenic - it moves 3 Na+ out for 2 K+ in, contributing about -4 mV directly. Ouabain and digoxin inhibit it.`,`Myelin raises conduction velocity by lowering membrane capacitance and raising resistance; internodal distance is about 1 to 2 mm and each jump takes roughly 20 microseconds.`,`Demyelination (multiple sclerosis, Guillain-Barre syndrome) slows or blocks conduction without cutting the axon.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Tract`,`Carries`,`Where it crosses`],rows:[[`Lateral corticospinal`,`Voluntary motor`,`Pyramids of medulla`],[`Dorsal column - medial lemniscus`,`Fine touch, vibration, proprioception`,`Medulla (internal arcuate fibres)`],[`Lateral spinothalamic`,`Pain and temperature`,`Cord, 1 to 2 segments above entry`],[`Anterior spinothalamic`,`Crude touch and pressure`,`Cord, within a few segments`],[`Spinocerebellar`,`Unconscious proprioception`,`Dorsal tract uncrossed`]],caption:`Because pain fibres cross in the cord and dorsal columns cross in the medulla, a hemisection of the cord (Brown-Sequard) gives ipsilateral loss of touch and proprioception with contralateral loss of pain and temperature.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Blood-brain barrier: tight junctions between capillary endothelial cells plus astrocyte foot processes. Lipophilic, unionised, low molecular weight drugs cross best; levodopa crosses on the large neutral amino acid carrier while dopamine cannot cross at all.`,`Circumventricular organs - area postrema (chemoreceptor trigger zone), median eminence, subfornical organ, pineal - lie outside the barrier, which is why apomorphine can trigger vomiting.`,`CSF is formed at about 500 mL per day by the choroid plexus, circulates through the ventricles and is absorbed by arachnoid villi into the dural venous sinuses. Acetazolamide reduces its formation.`,`Basal ganglia: loss of dopaminergic nigrostriatal neurons causes Parkinson disease (rigidity, tremor, bradykinesia); degeneration of striatal GABAergic neurons causes Huntington chorea.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Receptor`,`Coupling`,`Main effect`],rows:[[`Nicotinic N-M`,`Na+/K+ ion channel`,`Skeletal muscle contraction`],[`Muscarinic M2`,`Gi, K+ channel opening`,`Slows heart rate`],[`Muscarinic M3`,`Gq, IP3 and DAG`,`Gland secretion, smooth muscle contraction`],[`Alpha 1`,`Gq, IP3 and DAG`,`Vasoconstriction, mydriasis`],[`Alpha 2`,`Gi, cAMP falls`,`Presynaptic inhibition of NA release`],[`Beta 1 and beta 2`,`Gs, cAMP rises`,`Cardiac stimulation; bronchodilation`],[`GABA-A`,`Cl- ion channel`,`Fast inhibition; site of benzodiazepines`],[`NMDA`,`Ca2+ permeable channel`,`Learning, memory, excitotoxicity`]]},{kind:`para`,text:`Phototransduction is an inhibitory cascade. In the dark a cGMP-gated cation current keeps the photoreceptor depolarised and releasing glutamate. Light isomerises 11-cis retinal to all-trans retinal, activating transducin, which activates phosphodiesterase; cGMP falls, the channel closes and the cell hyperpolarises. The photoreceptor is therefore the rare receptor that responds to its stimulus by hyperpolarising.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Rods versus cones`,text:`Rods are more numerous (about 120 million) and far more sensitive but give no colour and poor acuity; cones number about 6 million, are concentrated at the fovea and need bright light. Vitamin A deficiency hits rods first, giving night blindness.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`High-yield numbers`,text:`Adult brain weight about 1400 g; brain uses 20 percent of resting oxygen; normal CSF pressure 60 to 150 mm H2O; sound is amplified about 22 times by the ossicular lever and area ratio; human hearing range 20 to 20000 Hz.`}]},{topicId:`ap-digestive`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The digestive system is a long tube from mouth to anus, about 9 metres in an adult, plus glands that pour secretions into it. Its job is to break food into molecules small enough to be absorbed into blood.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The route food takes`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Mouth - teeth chew food, saliva wets it and starts starch digestion.`,`Pharynx and oesophagus - a 25 cm muscular tube pushes the bolus down by peristalsis.`,`Stomach - a J-shaped bag that stores food, churns it and adds acid and pepsin, turning it into chyme.`,`Small intestine - duodenum, jejunum and ileum, about 6 metres, where most digestion and nearly all absorption happen.`,`Large intestine - caecum, colon, rectum, about 1.5 metres, absorbs water and salts and forms faeces.`,`Anus - the two sphincters, one involuntary and one voluntary, control defaecation.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`The wall of the tube has four layers everywhere: mucosa (inner lining), submucosa (vessels and Meissner nerve plexus), muscularis externa (inner circular and outer longitudinal muscle with the Auerbach plexus between them) and serosa (outer covering).`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The helper glands`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Salivary glands: parotid, submandibular and sublingual. They make about 1 to 1.5 litres of saliva a day.`,`Liver: the largest gland, about 1.5 kg, makes bile and processes everything absorbed from the gut.`,`Gall bladder: a small sac under the liver that stores and concentrates bile.`,`Pancreas: makes digestive enzymes for all three food types, plus insulin and glucagon for blood sugar.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Food type`,`Broken down into`],rows:[[`Carbohydrate (starch, sugars)`,`Glucose, galactose, fructose`],[`Protein`,`Amino acids and small peptides`],[`Fat (triglyceride)`,`Fatty acids and monoglycerides`],[`Nucleic acid`,`Nucleotides and nucleosides`]]},{kind:`para`,text:`Absorption happens mostly in the small intestine, whose surface is hugely enlarged by circular folds, finger-like villi and microvilli on each cell (the brush border). Sugars and amino acids go into blood capillaries and then to the liver by the hepatic portal vein; most fats go into lymph vessels called lacteals.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`One-line recall`,text:`Vitamin B12 needs intrinsic factor from stomach parietal cells and is absorbed in the terminal ileum; iron is absorbed in the duodenum; bile salts are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Small intestine order`,text:`Dow Jones Index: Duodenum, Jejunum, Ileum.`}]},{topicId:`ap-digestive`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The exam version of this topic is the secretion table - which juice contains which enzyme, acting on which substrate, giving which product - together with the three GIT hormones and the functions of the liver.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Digestive secretions`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Secretion (pH)`,`Enzyme`,`Substrate`,`Product`],rows:[[`Saliva (6.8)`,`Ptyalin (salivary amylase)`,`Starch`,`Maltose, dextrins`],[`Saliva`,`Lingual lipase`,`Triglyceride`,`Fatty acid, diglyceride`],[`Gastric juice (1-2)`,`Pepsin (from pepsinogen)`,`Protein`,`Proteoses, peptones`],[`Gastric juice`,`Gastric lipase, rennin`,`Fat; casein of milk`,`Fatty acids; curd`],[`Pancreatic juice (8)`,`Trypsin, chymotrypsin`,`Protein, peptones`,`Small peptides`],[`Pancreatic juice`,`Carboxypeptidase`,`Peptide C-terminal`,`Amino acids`],[`Pancreatic juice`,`Pancreatic amylase`,`Starch, glycogen`,`Maltose, isomaltose`],[`Pancreatic juice`,`Pancreatic lipase + colipase`,`Triglyceride`,`2-monoglyceride, 2 fatty acids`],[`Succus entericus (7.5)`,`Maltase, sucrase, lactase`,`Disaccharides`,`Monosaccharides`],[`Succus entericus`,`Aminopeptidase, dipeptidase`,`Peptides`,`Amino acids`],[`Succus entericus`,`Enterokinase (enteropeptidase)`,`Trypsinogen`,`Trypsin`],[`Bile (7.6-8.6)`,`No enzyme; bile salts`,`Fat globules`,`Emulsified fat, micelles`]],caption:`Bile contains no digestive enzyme at all - it emulsifies, it does not hydrolyse.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Gastric secretion`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Parietal (oxyntic) cells: hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor. The H+/K+ ATPase proton pump is the target of omeprazole.`,`Chief (peptic) cells: pepsinogen, activated to pepsin by HCl and then autocatalytically.`,`Mucous neck cells and surface cells: mucus and bicarbonate, the mucosal barrier.`,`G cells of the antrum: gastrin. Enterochromaffin-like cells: histamine. D cells: somatostatin.`]},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Cephalic phase - about 20 percent of the response, triggered by sight, smell and taste through the vagus.`,`Gastric phase - about 70 percent, triggered by distension and peptides acting through gastrin.`,`Intestinal phase - about 10 percent, and largely inhibitory once chyme reaches the duodenum.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Hormone`,`Source`,`Stimulus`,`Action`],rows:[[`Gastrin`,`G cells, gastric antrum`,`Peptides, distension, vagus`,`HCl and pepsinogen secretion`],[`Secretin`,`S cells, duodenum`,`Acid chyme, pH below 4.5`,`Bicarbonate-rich pancreatic juice`],[`Cholecystokinin`,`I cells, duodenum`,`Fatty acids, amino acids`,`Enzyme-rich juice, gall bladder contraction`],[`GIP`,`K cells, duodenum`,`Glucose, fat`,`Insulin release, inhibits gastric acid`],[`Motilin`,`M cells, small intestine`,`Fasting state`,`Migrating motor complex`]],caption:`Secretin was the first hormone ever discovered, by Bayliss and Starling in 1902.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Liver and bile`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Metabolic: glycogenesis, glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, lipogenesis, ketogenesis, deamination and transamination.`,`Synthetic: albumin, all clotting factors except factor VIII, and most plasma globulins except immunoglobulins.`,`Excretory: bilirubin conjugation with glucuronic acid, and excretion of bile salts, cholesterol and drugs.`,`Detoxification: phase I oxidation by cytochrome P450 and phase II conjugation; also converts ammonia to urea.`,`Storage: glycogen, vitamins A, D, B12 and K, and iron as ferritin.`,`Bile is about 700 to 1200 mL a day; the gall bladder concentrates it 5 to 20 times by absorbing water and salt.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`Primary bile acids - cholic and chenodeoxycholic acid - are made from cholesterol in the liver and conjugated with glycine or taurine. Colonic bacteria convert them to the secondary bile acids deoxycholic and lithocholic acid. About 95 percent are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum and returned to the liver: the enterohepatic circulation, which recycles the pool 6 to 10 times a day.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Which enzyme is secreted active?`,text:`Proteases are all secreted as inactive zymogens (pepsinogen, trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase) to protect the gland itself. Amylase and lipase are secreted already active because they cannot digest the gland.`}]},{topicId:`ap-digestive`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced work centres on the transporters used in absorption, the mechanism of acid secretion, the regulation of pancreatic juice and the quantitative facts about GIT fluid handling.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Nutrient`,`Transporter`,`Mechanism`],rows:[[`Glucose, galactose`,`SGLT1 (apical)`,`Secondary active with Na+`],[`Fructose`,`GLUT5 (apical)`,`Facilitated diffusion`],[`All sugars (basolateral exit)`,`GLUT2`,`Facilitated diffusion`],[`Amino acids`,`Na+-dependent carriers`,`Secondary active`],[`Di- and tripeptides`,`PepT1`,`H+-coupled cotransport`],[`Fatty acids, monoglycerides`,`Micelle then diffusion`,`Passive across brush border`]],caption:`PepT1 also carries beta-lactam antibiotics and ACE inhibitors, which explains their good oral absorption.`},{kind:`para`,text:`In the parietal cell, carbonic anhydrase makes carbonic acid from CO2 and water. H+ is pumped into the lumen by the H+/K+ ATPase in exchange for K+, while the HCO3- left behind leaves across the basolateral membrane in exchange for Cl-. The bicarbonate entering venous blood after a meal is the alkaline tide, which can raise gastric venous pH measurably.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Three secretagogues act on the parietal cell: acetylcholine on M3 and gastrin on CCK-B raise intracellular Ca2+, while histamine on H2 raises cAMP. All three converge on the proton pump, which is why a proton pump inhibitor blocks acid more completely than an H2 blocker.`,`Pancreatic ductal cells secrete the bicarbonate under secretin control; acinar cells secrete the enzymes under CCK and vagal control. Enzyme content of the juice therefore tracks CCK, and volume and alkalinity track secretin.`,`Trypsin inhibitor in pancreatic juice and the storage of enzymes in zymogen granules prevent autodigestion; failure of these safeguards underlies acute pancreatitis.`,`Fat absorption needs micelles about 4 to 6 nm across formed by bile salts above their critical micelle concentration; inside the enterocyte, triglyceride is resynthesised and packaged with apoB-48 into chylomicrons that leave by lymph.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Daily GIT fluid: 2 L intake + 7 L secretions = 9 L presented; 8 L absorbed by small intestine, 0.9 L by colon, 0.1 L lost in faeces`,caption:`This is why secretory diarrhoea dehydrates so fast - only a small fraction of the load has to escape absorption.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Interstitial cells of Cajal are the pacemakers of the gut, generating slow waves at about 3 per minute in the stomach, 12 per minute in the duodenum and 8 per minute in the ileum.`,`The migrating motor complex sweeps the fasting gut every 90 to 120 minutes under motilin control; erythromycin is a motilin agonist and so is used as a prokinetic.`,`Vomiting is coordinated by the vomiting centre in the medulla, which receives input from the chemoreceptor trigger zone in the area postrema - outside the blood-brain barrier, hence sensitive to circulating emetics such as apomorphine and cytotoxics.`,`Gastric emptying is slowed by fat, acid and hypertonicity in the duodenum through the enterogastric reflex and by CCK; liquids empty faster than solids.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Numbers worth memorising`,text:`Gastric juice 2 to 3 L/day at pH 1 to 2; pancreatic juice about 1.5 L/day at pH 8; bile 700 to 1200 mL/day; succus entericus 1.8 L/day; total absorptive area of small intestine about 200 square metres.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Portal blood does not carry fat`,text:`Short and medium chain fatty acids do enter portal blood, but long chain fatty acids are re-esterified and leave as chylomicrons in lymph, bypassing the liver on first pass. This is the basis of using medium chain triglycerides in malabsorption.`}]},{topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The kidneys clean the blood and make urine; the endocrine glands control the body slowly using chemicals called hormones carried in blood. This level defines the parts of a nephron, how urine is made, and what each gland secretes.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The nephron`},{kind:`para`,text:`Each kidney holds about a million nephrons, the working units. A nephron begins as a knot of capillaries called the glomerulus sitting inside a cup, the Bowman capsule. From the capsule the fluid runs through the proximal convoluted tubule, then the U-shaped loop of Henle, then the distal convoluted tubule, and finally into a collecting duct.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Afferent arteriole: brings blood into the glomerulus. It is wider than the efferent arteriole leaving it, which keeps pressure high.`,`Glomerulus plus Bowman capsule together are called the renal corpuscle or Malpighian body.`,`Cortex: the outer part of the kidney holding the corpuscles and convoluted tubules.`,`Medulla: the inner part holding the loops of Henle and collecting ducts, arranged in pyramids.`,`Juxtaglomerular apparatus: a small cluster of cells where the distal tubule touches its own afferent arteriole; it secretes renin.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`How urine is made`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Filtration - pressure in the glomerulus pushes water and small molecules into the capsule. Blood cells and plasma proteins are too big and stay behind. About 180 litres are filtered a day.`,`Reabsorption - the tubules take back what the body needs: all the glucose, most water, salt and amino acids. About 178.5 litres go back.`,`Secretion - the tubules add unwanted things such as H+, K+, ammonia and many drugs into the urine.`,`The remaining 1 to 1.5 litres a day leaves as urine.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The endocrine glands`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Gland`,`A main hormone`,`What it does`],rows:[[`Pituitary`,`Growth hormone`,`Growth of bones and tissues`],[`Thyroid`,`Thyroxine (T4)`,`Sets the speed of metabolism`],[`Parathyroid`,`Parathormone`,`Raises blood calcium`],[`Adrenal cortex`,`Cortisol`,`Stress and glucose control`],[`Adrenal medulla`,`Adrenaline`,`Fight or flight response`],[`Pancreas`,`Insulin`,`Lowers blood glucose`],[`Ovary`,`Oestrogen`,`Female secondary sex characters`],[`Testis`,`Testosterone`,`Male secondary sex characters`],[`Pineal`,`Melatonin`,`Sleep and wake rhythm`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Anterior pituitary hormones`,text:`FLAT PiG - FSH, LH, ACTH, TSH (the tropic ones) and Prolactin, GH (the direct ones).`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Straight recall`,text:`Normal urine output 1 to 1.5 L/day; urine pH about 6 (range 4.5 to 8); GFR about 125 mL/min; renal blood flow about 1200 mL/min, which is 20 to 25 percent of cardiac output.`}]},{topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`This is the level where most GPAT marks sit: which segment handles which substance, the pressures that set GFR, the counter-current mechanism, the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, and the full hormone-disorder table.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Segment by segment`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Segment`,`Reabsorbed`,`Secreted`],rows:[[`Proximal convoluted tubule`,`65% Na+ and water, 100% glucose and amino acids, 85% HCO3-`,`H+, organic acids and bases, drugs`],[`Descending limb of Henle`,`Water only (permeable to water, not solute)`,`Nil`],[`Thin and thick ascending limb`,`Na+, K+, 2Cl- by NKCC2; water impermeable`,`Nil`],[`Distal convoluted tubule`,`Na+ and Cl- by NCC; Ca2+ under PTH`,`K+, H+`],[`Collecting duct`,`Water via aquaporin-2 under ADH; Na+ under aldosterone`,`K+, H+, NH3`]],caption:`Loop diuretics block NKCC2, thiazides block NCC, and spironolactone blocks the aldosterone receptor in the collecting duct.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Net filtration pressure = Glomerular capillary pressure - (Capsular pressure + Plasma oncotic pressure)`,caption:`Typically 60 - (18 + 32) = about 10 mm Hg. Filtration stops if capsular pressure rises (obstruction) or if oncotic pressure rises (dehydration).`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`GFR = Kf x Net filtration pressure; Clearance of X = (Ux x V) / Px`,caption:`Kf is the filtration coefficient. Ux is urine concentration, V urine flow rate in mL/min, Px plasma concentration. Inulin clearance equals GFR because inulin is freely filtered but neither reabsorbed nor secreted.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Autoregulation keeps GFR steady between mean arterial pressures of about 80 and 180 mm Hg by the myogenic response and tubuloglomerular feedback from the macula densa.`,`Constricting the afferent arteriole lowers GFR; constricting the efferent arteriole (as angiotensin II does) raises it. This is why an ACE inhibitor can drop GFR in bilateral renal artery stenosis.`,`Filtration fraction = GFR / renal plasma flow, normally about 125/625 = 0.20.`,`Renal threshold for glucose is about 180 mg/dL of plasma; transport maximum is about 375 mg/min in men and 300 mg/min in women.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Counter-current mechanism`},{kind:`para`,text:`The loop of Henle is a counter-current multiplier and the vasa recta a counter-current exchanger. The thick ascending limb pumps NaCl out without water following, so the interstitium becomes hypertonic - up to 1200 mOsm/kg at the tip of the medulla - while tubular fluid leaving the loop is hypotonic at about 100 mOsm/kg. Urea recycling from the inner medullary collecting duct contributes roughly half of that gradient. The collecting duct then passes through this gradient, and how much water leaves it depends entirely on ADH.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`RAAS and acid-base`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Fall in renal perfusion, low tubular NaCl at the macula densa, or sympathetic beta 1 stimulation makes juxtaglomerular cells release renin.`,`Renin converts angiotensinogen from the liver into angiotensin I.`,`ACE, mainly in the lung, converts angiotensin I into angiotensin II.`,`Angiotensin II constricts arterioles, stimulates thirst and ADH, and releases aldosterone from the zona glomerulosa.`,`Aldosterone makes the collecting duct retain Na+ and water and excrete K+ and H+, restoring blood pressure.`]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The kidney regulates pH in three ways: reclaiming filtered bicarbonate in the proximal tubule, excreting H+ buffered by phosphate as titratable acid, and excreting H+ as ammonium.`,`Ammonium excretion is the adaptable route - it can rise several fold over days in chronic acidosis, which is why renal compensation is slow but powerful.`,`Minimum urine pH achievable is about 4.5; below this the H+ gradient cannot be maintained.`]},{kind:`para`,text:`Micturition: the bladder fills at low pressure because of receptive relaxation. At about 300 to 400 mL, stretch receptors fire and afferents run to S2-S4. Parasympathetic (pelvic nerve) output contracts the detrusor and relaxes the internal sphincter, while the somatic pudendal nerve is voluntarily inhibited to open the external sphincter. The cortex can hold the reflex in check until it is convenient.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Endocrine glands, hormones and disorders`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Hormone (gland)`,`Function`,`Excess`,`Deficiency`],rows:[[`GH (anterior pituitary)`,`Growth, protein anabolism`,`Gigantism, acromegaly`,`Dwarfism`],[`ACTH (anterior pituitary)`,`Drives adrenal cortex`,`Cushing disease`,`Secondary adrenal failure`],[`TSH (anterior pituitary)`,`Drives thyroid`,`Hyperthyroidism`,`Secondary hypothyroidism`],[`Prolactin (anterior pituitary)`,`Lactation`,`Galactorrhoea, infertility`,`Failure of lactation`],[`ADH (posterior pituitary)`,`Water reabsorption`,`SIADH, hyponatraemia`,`Diabetes insipidus`],[`Oxytocin (posterior pituitary)`,`Uterine contraction, milk ejection`,`Uterine hyperstimulation`,`Poor milk let-down`],[`T3 and T4 (thyroid)`,`Basal metabolic rate`,`Graves disease, thyrotoxicosis`,`Cretinism, myxoedema`],[`Calcitonin (thyroid C cells)`,`Lowers plasma calcium`,`Usually asymptomatic`,`No defined syndrome`],[`PTH (parathyroid)`,`Raises calcium, lowers phosphate`,`Osteitis fibrosa cystica, stones`,`Hypocalcaemic tetany`],[`Cortisol (adrenal cortex)`,`Gluconeogenesis, anti-inflammatory`,`Cushing syndrome`,`Addison disease`],[`Aldosterone (adrenal cortex)`,`Na+ retention, K+ loss`,`Conn syndrome, hypokalaemia`,`Hyperkalaemia, hypotension`],[`Adrenaline (adrenal medulla)`,`Fight or flight`,`Phaeochromocytoma`,`No defined syndrome`],[`Insulin (beta cells)`,`Lowers blood glucose`,`Hypoglycaemia, insulinoma`,`Diabetes mellitus`],[`Glucagon (alpha cells)`,`Raises blood glucose`,`Glucagonoma`,`Fasting hypoglycaemia`],[`Testosterone (testis)`,`Male sex characters`,`Precocious puberty`,`Eunuchoidism`],[`Oestrogen (ovary)`,`Female sex characters`,`Endometrial hyperplasia`,`Amenorrhoea, osteoporosis`],[`Melatonin (pineal)`,`Circadian rhythm`,`Delayed puberty`,`Sleep disturbance`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`The posterior pituitary makes nothing`,text:`ADH and oxytocin are synthesised in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus and merely stored and released by the posterior pituitary, which is nervous tissue, not glandular.`}]},{topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The high-scoring detail here is quantitative clearance, the transport maxima, the exact zones of the adrenal cortex, and the second-messenger class of each hormone.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Effective renal plasma flow = clearance of PAH; Renal blood flow = ERPF / (1 - haematocrit)`,caption:`PAH is filtered and almost completely secreted in one pass, so its clearance (about 625 mL/min) estimates plasma flow. With a haematocrit of 0.45, RBF is about 1136 mL/min.`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Free water clearance CH2O = V - Cosm, where Cosm = (Uosm x V) / Posm`,caption:`A positive value means dilute urine is being made (water diuresis, diabetes insipidus); a negative value means water is being conserved (high ADH, SIADH).`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Filtration through the glomerular barrier is both size and charge selective. The barrier is fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane rich in negatively charged heparan sulphate, and podocyte slit diaphragms. Loss of the negative charge lets albumin through even though its size is borderline - the lesion in minimal change disease.`,`Substances with molecular weight below about 7000 daltons filter freely; above about 70000 daltons filtration is essentially zero. Only unbound drug is filtered, so a highly protein-bound drug has a low filtration clearance.`,`The proximal tubule secretes organic anions (penicillin, probenecid, methotrexate, PAH) by OAT transporters and organic cations (quinine, cimetidine, metformin) by OCT transporters. Probenecid competes with penicillin for OAT and so prolongs its half-life.`,`Erythropoietin comes from peritubular interstitial fibroblasts of the cortex, and the kidney also performs the 1-alpha-hydroxylation that makes calcitriol - hence the anaemia and the bone disease of chronic kidney disease.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Adrenal zone`,`Product`,`Chief control`],rows:[[`Zona glomerulosa`,`Aldosterone`,`Angiotensin II and plasma K+`],[`Zona fasciculata`,`Cortisol`,`ACTH`],[`Zona reticularis`,`Androgens (DHEA)`,`ACTH`],[`Medulla`,`Adrenaline 80%, noradrenaline 20%`,`Preganglionic sympathetic fibres`]],caption:`Mnemonic GFR: Glomerulosa, Fasciculata, Reticularis making Salt, Sugar, Sex - the deeper you go the sweeter it gets.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Hormone class`,`Signalling route`,`Examples`],rows:[[`Steroid`,`Cytoplasmic or nuclear receptor, gene transcription`,`Cortisol, aldosterone, oestrogen`],[`Thyroid hormone`,`Nuclear receptor`,`T3, T4`],[`Peptide via Gs and cAMP`,`Adenylyl cyclase, protein kinase A`,`ACTH, LH, FSH, TSH, glucagon, PTH, ADH V2`],[`Peptide via Gq and IP3`,`Phospholipase C, Ca2+ release`,`ADH V1, oxytocin, TRH, GnRH, angiotensin II`],[`Tyrosine kinase receptor`,`Autophosphorylation, IRS pathway`,`Insulin, IGF-1`],[`JAK-STAT`,`Cytokine receptor associated kinase`,`Growth hormone, prolactin`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`T4 is the main secreted product but T3 is the active hormone; about 80 percent of circulating T3 comes from peripheral 5-prime deiodination of T4. Propylthiouracil blocks both thyroid peroxidase and this peripheral conversion, whereas carbimazole blocks only the gland.`,`Reverse T3 is inactive and rises in starvation and severe illness, the euthyroid sick state.`,`Calcium is raised by PTH (bone resorption, distal tubular reabsorption, renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase) and calcitriol (gut absorption), and lowered by calcitonin. Plasma calcium is 9 to 11 mg/dL, about half of it ionised.`,`Alkalosis lowers ionised calcium by increasing binding to albumin, which is why hyperventilation can produce carpopedal spasm with a normal total calcium.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Values examiners love`,text:`Filtered load of Na+ about 25000 mEq/day with more than 99 percent reabsorbed; maximum urine osmolality 1200 to 1400 mOsm/kg and minimum about 50 mOsm/kg; obligatory urine volume about 500 mL/day.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Aldosterone escape`,text:`Continued aldosterone excess does not cause endless oedema. Volume expansion raises atrial natriuretic peptide and pressure natriuresis, so sodium excretion resets - Conn syndrome presents with hypertension and hypokalaemia but characteristically without oedema.`}]},{topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Bones give the body its frame, joints let it bend, muscles pull on bones to move them, and the reproductive organs make the cells and hormones needed to produce a child. This level defines each part.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The skeleton`},{kind:`para`,text:`An adult has 206 bones. The axial skeleton (80 bones) is the skull, vertebral column, ribs and sternum. The appendicular skeleton (126 bones) is the limbs with their girdles. Bone itself is a connective tissue made of collagen fibres hardened with calcium phosphate as hydroxyapatite crystals.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Compact bone: the dense outer shell, built of cylinders called Haversian systems or osteons.`,`Spongy (cancellous) bone: the light honeycomb inside the ends of long bones, filled with marrow.`,`Osteoblast: the cell that builds new bone. Osteocyte: a mature bone cell trapped in a lacuna. Osteoclast: the large multinucleate cell that dissolves bone.`,`Periosteum: the tough membrane covering the outside of a bone. Endosteum lines the marrow cavity.`,`Shapes of bone: long (femur), short (carpals), flat (skull, sternum), irregular (vertebra), sesamoid (patella).`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Joints`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type`,`Movement`,`Example`],rows:[[`Fibrous (synarthrosis)`,`None`,`Skull sutures`],[`Cartilaginous (amphiarthrosis)`,`Slight`,`Intervertebral discs, pubic symphysis`],[`Synovial (diarthrosis)`,`Free`,`Knee, shoulder, hip`]]},{kind:`para`,text:`A synovial joint has a cavity holding slippery synovial fluid, articular cartilage on the bone ends, and a capsule with ligaments around it. Ball and socket joints (shoulder, hip) move in every direction; hinge joints (elbow, knee) move in one plane; pivot joints (atlas on axis) rotate.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Muscle and reproduction`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Skeletal muscle is striped, attached to bones and under voluntary control.`,`Smooth muscle is unstriped, found in gut, vessels and bladder, and is involuntary.`,`Cardiac muscle is striped but involuntary, found only in the heart, and its cells are joined by intercalated discs.`,`Male organs: two testes make sperm and testosterone; the sperm travel through epididymis, vas deferens and urethra. The prostate and seminal vesicles add fluid to make semen.`,`Female organs: two ovaries release one egg a month; the fallopian tube carries it to the uterus, where a fertilised egg implants and grows.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Straight recall`,text:`206 bones in an adult and about 270 at birth; the femur is the longest bone and the stapes the smallest; there are 33 vertebrae (7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 5 fused sacral, 4 fused coccygeal) and 12 pairs of ribs.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Vertebrae count`,text:`Breakfast at 7, lunch at 12, dinner at 5 - cervical 7, thoracic 12, lumbar 5.`}]},{topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The exam level is the three-muscle comparison table, the sliding filament sequence, the neuromuscular junction as a drug target, and the hormonal control of the menstrual cycle.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Bone formation`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Intramembranous ossification lays bone straight down in a fibrous membrane - flat bones of the skull, clavicle and mandible.`,`Endochondral ossification replaces a hyaline cartilage model - all long bones. The primary centre appears in the shaft, secondary centres in the epiphyses.`,`The epiphyseal plate is where a long bone lengthens; it closes under oestrogen at puberty, which is why early puberty shortens final height.`,`Bone remodelling is continuous: osteoclasts resorb under RANKL and PTH, osteoblasts rebuild; oestrogen and calcitonin restrain resorption, which is why bone is lost after menopause.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Comparing the three muscles`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Skeletal`,`Smooth`,`Cardiac`],rows:[[`Striations`,`Present`,`Absent`,`Present`],[`Nucleus`,`Many, peripheral`,`One, central`,`One or two, central`],[`Control`,`Voluntary`,`Involuntary`,`Involuntary`],[`Cell junction`,`None`,`Gap junctions`,`Intercalated discs`],[`Ca2+ source`,`Sarcoplasmic reticulum`,`Mainly extracellular`,`Both, calcium-induced release`],[`Ca2+ receptor`,`Troponin C`,`Calmodulin`,`Troponin C`],[`T tubules`,`At A-I junction, well developed`,`Absent (caveolae instead)`,`At Z line, wide`],[`Fatigue`,`Fatigues`,`Does not fatigue`,`Does not fatigue`],[`Pacemaker`,`None`,`Present in some`,`SA node`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Sliding filament mechanism`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`The action potential runs along the sarcolemma and down the T tubule.`,`The dihydropyridine receptor senses the voltage and opens the ryanodine receptor of the sarcoplasmic reticulum, releasing Ca2+.`,`Ca2+ binds troponin C; the troponin-tropomyosin complex shifts and uncovers the myosin binding site on actin.`,`The energised myosin head attaches to actin, forming a cross-bridge.`,`ADP and Pi are released and the head swivels - the power stroke - sliding actin over myosin.`,`A fresh ATP binds myosin and detaches it; ATP hydrolysis re-cocks the head for the next cycle.`,`Ca2+ is pumped back by SERCA, tropomyosin recovers the site and the muscle relaxes.`]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The sarcomere runs from Z line to Z line. The A band (myosin) keeps its length during contraction; the I band and H zone shorten. Only the degree of overlap changes - the filaments themselves do not shorten.`,`Rigor mortis occurs because ATP runs out, so myosin cannot detach from actin.`,`Isotonic contraction shortens the muscle at constant tension; isometric contraction raises tension without change in length.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Neuromuscular junction`},{kind:`para`,text:`The motor nerve terminal releases acetylcholine, which crosses a 50 nm cleft and binds nicotinic N-M receptors on the junctional folds of the motor end plate. Sodium entry produces an end plate potential large enough that a single nerve impulse always fires the muscle fibre - a safety factor of about 3 to 4. Acetylcholinesterase in the cleft hydrolyses the transmitter within about a millisecond.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Agent`,`Site`,`Effect`],rows:[[`Botulinum toxin`,`Presynaptic SNARE proteins`,`Blocks ACh release, flaccid paralysis`],[`Hemicholinium`,`Choline uptake`,`Depletes ACh stores`],[`d-Tubocurarine`,`Nicotinic receptor`,`Competitive block, non-depolarising`],[`Succinylcholine`,`Nicotinic receptor`,`Persistent depolarisation, phase I block`],[`Neostigmine`,`Acetylcholinesterase`,`Raises ACh, reverses curare block`],[`Dantrolene`,`Ryanodine receptor`,`Blocks Ca2+ release, treats malignant hyperthermia`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Reproduction`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Spermatogenesis takes about 74 days in the seminiferous tubules. Sertoli cells nurse the germ cells and secrete inhibin under FSH; Leydig cells in the interstitium secrete testosterone under LH. One primary spermatocyte gives four spermatozoa.`,`Oogenesis begins before birth. The oocyte is arrested in prophase I until ovulation, completes meiosis I to give a secondary oocyte and a polar body, then arrests in metaphase II until fertilisation. One oogonium gives one ovum and three polar bodies.`,`Semen volume is 2 to 5 mL with 60 to 150 million sperm per mL; below about 15 million per mL is oligospermia.`,`Fertilisation occurs in the ampulla of the fallopian tube; implantation of the blastocyst occurs about 6 to 7 days later.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Phase (28-day cycle)`,`Days`,`Dominant hormone`,`Ovary and uterus`],rows:[[`Menstrual`,`1-5`,`All low`,`Endometrium shed`],[`Follicular / proliferative`,`6-13`,`FSH then oestrogen`,`Follicle grows, endometrium thickens`],[`Ovulation`,`14`,`LH surge`,`Graafian follicle ruptures`],[`Luteal / secretory`,`15-28`,`Progesterone from corpus luteum`,`Glands secrete, ready for implantation`]],caption:`The luteal phase is fixed at about 14 days; cycle length varies because the follicular phase varies.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Oestrogen feedback flips`,text:`Oestrogen normally exerts negative feedback, but sustained high oestrogen for about 36 hours in the late follicular phase switches to positive feedback and triggers the LH surge. This is the one physiological positive feedback loop in the endocrine system besides oxytocin in labour.`}]},{topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced detail: fibre typing, the length-tension relationship, latch bridges in smooth muscle, and the hormone timeline of pregnancy, parturition and lactation.`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Type I (slow oxidative)`,`Type IIb (fast glycolytic)`],rows:[[`Colour`,`Red, myoglobin rich`,`White, myoglobin poor`],[`Mitochondria`,`Many`,`Few`],[`Main ATP source`,`Oxidative phosphorylation`,`Anaerobic glycolysis`],[`Contraction speed`,`Slow`,`Fast`],[`Fatigue`,`Resistant`,`Rapid`],[`Example`,`Postural muscles, soleus`,`Extraocular, gastrocnemius`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The length-tension curve peaks at optimal sarcomere length, about 2.0 to 2.2 micrometres, where actin-myosin overlap is maximal. Tension falls at shorter lengths from filament interference and at longer lengths from lost overlap.`,`The force-velocity relationship is hyperbolic: velocity of shortening is maximal at zero load and zero at the isometric maximum.`,`Motor unit recruitment follows the size principle - small, slow, fatigue-resistant units fire first, then larger fast units as force demand rises. Summation of twitches gives incomplete then complete tetanus.`,`Smooth muscle uses myosin light chain kinase activated by Ca2+-calmodulin; dephosphorylation by myosin light chain phosphatase relaxes it. The latch state lets smooth muscle maintain tone at very low ATP cost, which is why a sphincter can stay closed for hours.`]},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Creatine phosphate + ADP -> ATP + creatine (creatine kinase); usable ATP store supports only 2 to 3 seconds of maximal work`,caption:`Creatine phosphate extends this to about 10 seconds, after which glycolysis and then oxidative phosphorylation take over. Oxygen debt is the extra oxygen consumed afterwards to clear lactate and restore stores.`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Fertilisation and cleavage; the blastocyst implants about day 6 to 7 after ovulation.`,`The syncytiotrophoblast secretes hCG, which rescues the corpus luteum so progesterone does not fall and menstruation does not occur. hCG peaks at about 8 to 10 weeks.`,`From about the tenth week the placenta itself takes over progesterone and oestrogen production - the luteo-placental shift.`,`Human placental lactogen rises steadily, causing insulin resistance and mobilising maternal fat - the basis of gestational diabetes.`,`Near term, oxytocin receptors on the myometrium multiply and the oestrogen to progesterone ratio rises, so the uterus becomes excitable.`,`Labour is driven by positive feedback: cervical stretch releases oxytocin, which contracts the uterus and stretches the cervix further - the Ferguson reflex. Prostaglandins E2 and F2 alpha ripen the cervix.`]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Lactation involves two separate hormones. Prolactin drives milk synthesis and is held in check throughout pregnancy by high oestrogen and progesterone; their fall after delivery lets milk secretion begin. Oxytocin drives milk ejection by contracting myoepithelial cells - the let-down reflex, which is neuroendocrine and can be inhibited by stress.`,`Prolactin also suppresses GnRH, giving lactational amenorrhoea; dopamine from the hypothalamus is the tonic inhibitor of prolactin, so dopamine antagonists cause galactorrhoea and bromocriptine suppresses lactation.`,`Colostrum, secreted for the first 2 to 4 days, is rich in protein and secretory IgA but low in fat and lactose compared with mature milk.`,`Testosterone is converted by 5-alpha-reductase to dihydrotestosterone, which mediates prostatic growth and male pattern baldness, and by aromatase to oestradiol, which mediates epiphyseal closure in both sexes.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Numbers that get asked`,text:`Basal body temperature rises about 0.5 degrees C after ovulation because of progesterone; pregnancy lasts about 280 days from the last menstrual period; the corpus luteum survives about 14 days without hCG.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Calcium in smooth versus skeletal muscle`,text:`Skeletal muscle Ca2+ acts on troponin C, a thin filament protein, and comes almost entirely from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Smooth muscle has no troponin at all - Ca2+ acts on calmodulin to regulate the thick filament, and much of it enters from outside, which is why calcium channel blockers relax vascular smooth muscle but do not weaken skeletal muscle.`}]}],t=[{id:`q-ap-cell-tissue-1`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The organelle that contains acid hydrolases and is known as the suicide bag of the cell is the`,options:[`Lysosome`,`Golgi apparatus`,`Smooth endoplasmic reticulum`,`Peroxisome`],answer:0,explanation:`Lysosomes hold about 40 acid hydrolases active at pH 5 and can digest the cell itself, hence the name suicide bag. Peroxisomes are the usual distractor, but they contain oxidases and catalase for beta-oxidation and hydrogen peroxide disposal, not acid hydrolases.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ap-cell-tissue-2`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Blood is classified as which type of tissue?`,options:[`Nervous`,`Epithelial`,`Connective`,`Muscular`],answer:2,explanation:`Blood is a connective tissue: it arises from mesoderm and consists of cells suspended in an extracellular matrix, the plasma. Students often reject this because blood is fluid, but a fluid matrix does not disqualify a tissue from being connective.`},{id:`q-ap-cell-tissue-3`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The Na+/K+ ATPase moves ions in the ratio`,options:[`2 Na+ out for 3 K+ in`,`3 Na+ out for 2 K+ in`,`1 Na+ out for 1 K+ in`,`3 Na+ in for 2 K+ out`],answer:1,explanation:`The pump extrudes 3 sodium ions and takes in 2 potassium ions per ATP hydrolysed, making it electrogenic and contributing directly to the negative resting potential. Reversing the ratio would move net positive charge inward and could not sustain the resting potential.`},{id:`q-ap-cell-tissue-4`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Transport of glucose into the intestinal epithelial cell against its concentration gradient using the sodium gradient is an example of`,options:[`Facilitated diffusion`,`Simple diffusion`,`Primary active transport`,`Secondary active transport`],answer:3,explanation:`SGLT1 does not hydrolyse ATP itself; it uses the sodium gradient created by the Na+/K+ ATPase, which makes it secondary active transport. It is not facilitated diffusion because glucose moves against its own gradient, and it is not primary active because no ATP is consumed at the carrier.`},{id:`q-ap-cell-tissue-5`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The fluid mosaic model of the plasma membrane was proposed by`,options:[`Robertson`,`Watson and Crick`,`Singer and Nicolson`,`Danielli and Davson`],answer:2,explanation:`Singer and Nicolson proposed the fluid mosaic model in 1972, with proteins floating in a fluid lipid bilayer. Danielli and Davson gave the earlier sandwich model in which protein layers coated both faces of the lipid, a model now known to be wrong.`},{id:`q-ap-cell-tissue-6`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Simple squamous epithelium lining the alveoli and forming the endothelium of blood vessels is best suited for`,options:[`Rapid diffusion and filtration`,`Absorption using microvilli`,`Secretion of enzymes`,`Protection against abrasion`],answer:0,explanation:`A single flattened layer gives the shortest possible diffusion path, which is what alveolar gas exchange and capillary filtration need. Protection against abrasion is the role of stratified squamous epithelium such as that of the oesophagus and skin.`},{id:`q-ap-cell-tissue-7`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which of the following is an example of a positive feedback mechanism in human physiology?`,options:[`Thyroid hormone suppression of TSH`,`Regulation of blood glucose by insulin`,`Baroreceptor control of blood pressure`,`Oxytocin release during labour`],answer:3,explanation:`Cervical stretch releases oxytocin, which contracts the uterus and stretches the cervix further, amplifying the response until delivery ends it. All the other options are classical negative feedback loops in which the output opposes the initiating change.`},{id:`q-ap-cell-tissue-8`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A drug that is a weak base with pKa 8.4 is placed in plasma at pH 7.4. The ratio of unionised to ionised drug is`,options:[`100 : 1`,`1 : 10`,`10 : 1`,`1 : 100`],answer:1,explanation:`For a base, pH = pKa + log (unionised/ionised), so 7.4 = 8.4 + log R and log R = -1, giving a ratio of 1 : 10. Only the unionised fraction crosses the lipid bilayer, so this base is largely ionised and poorly diffusible at plasma pH; inverting the equation to get 10 : 1 is the standard error.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ap-blood-lymph-1`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The normal lifespan of a human erythrocyte in circulation is about`,options:[`120 days`,`180 days`,`20 days`,`60 days`],answer:0,explanation:`A red cell survives about 120 days before macrophages of the spleen and liver remove it. Platelets last 7 to 10 days and most granulocytes only hours to a few days, which are the figures usually confused with it.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ap-blood-lymph-2`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which plasma protein is chiefly responsible for the colloid osmotic (oncotic) pressure of blood?`,options:[`Alpha-1 antitrypsin`,`Fibrinogen`,`Albumin`,`Gamma globulin`],answer:2,explanation:`Albumin contributes roughly 80 percent of the oncotic pressure because it is both the most abundant plasma protein and the smallest, so it is present in the greatest molar concentration. Fibrinogen is far larger and present in much smaller amounts, so its osmotic contribution is negligible.`},{id:`q-ap-blood-lymph-3`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A person with blood group AB negative has`,options:[`D antigen present with anti-A antibody`,`Both A and B antigens, no anti-A or anti-B antibodies, no D antigen`,`No antigens and both antibodies`,`Both antigens and both antibodies`],answer:1,explanation:`Group AB carries both A and B agglutinogens on the red cell and therefore neither anti-A nor anti-B in plasma, making it the universal recipient for ABO; negative means the D antigen is absent. Saying both antigens and both antibodies are present is self-contradictory since the cells would agglutinate in their own plasma.`},{id:`q-ap-blood-lymph-4`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The prothrombin time chiefly tests the integrity of which pathway?`,options:[`Fibrinolytic pathway`,`Platelet adhesion`,`Intrinsic pathway`,`Extrinsic and common pathway`],answer:3,explanation:`Prothrombin time is initiated by tissue factor and therefore assesses factor VII plus the common pathway factors X, V, II and I; it is the test used to monitor warfarin. Activated partial thromboplastin time is the intrinsic pathway test and is used to monitor unfractionated heparin.`},{id:`q-ap-blood-lymph-5`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Vitamin K is required for the gamma-carboxylation of which set of clotting factors?`,options:[`VIII, IX, XI, XII`,`I, V, VIII, XIII`,`II, VII, IX, X`,`V, VIII, XI, XII`],answer:2,explanation:`Factors II, VII, IX and X, together with proteins C and S, need vitamin K dependent gamma-carboxylation of glutamate to bind calcium and phospholipid. Factor VIII is the classic wrong choice; it is not vitamin K dependent and is also the only clotting factor not made mainly in hepatocytes.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ap-blood-lymph-6`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The most abundant leucocyte in normal adult peripheral blood is the`,options:[`Neutrophil`,`Monocyte`,`Eosinophil`,`Lymphocyte`],answer:0,explanation:`Neutrophils make up 40 to 75 percent of circulating leucocytes in an adult and are the first responders in acute bacterial infection. Lymphocytes at 20 to 45 percent come second in adults, although they do predominate in young children.`},{id:`q-ap-blood-lymph-7`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A blood sample has haemoglobin 15 g/dL, packed cell volume 45 percent and red cell count 5 million per microlitre. The mean corpuscular haemoglobin concentration is`,options:[`90 fL`,`27 pg`,`30 g/dL`,`33.3 g/dL`],answer:3,explanation:`MCHC = (Hb x 100)/PCV = (15 x 100)/45 = 33.3 g/dL, which is normochromic. The value 90 fL is the MCV and 27 pg would be a plausible MCH, so the trap is picking an index with the wrong units.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ap-blood-lymph-8`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Haemolytic disease of the newborn due to Rh incompatibility typically spares the first pregnancy because`,options:[`Anti-D antibodies are neutralised by amniotic fluid`,`Maternal IgM formed first cannot cross the placenta`,`The foetal liver destroys maternal antibody`,`Rh antigen appears only after birth`],answer:1,explanation:`Primary sensitisation usually occurs at delivery, and the initial response is IgM, which is too large to cross the placenta; the secondary IgG response in a later pregnancy is what crosses and haemolyses foetal cells. Anti-D immunoglobulin given to the mother within 72 hours of delivery works by clearing foetal cells before sensitisation happens.`},{id:`q-ap-cardiovascular-1`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The pacemaker of the human heart is the`,options:[`Sinoatrial node`,`Bundle of His`,`Purkinje fibres`,`Atrioventricular node`],answer:0,explanation:`The sinoatrial node in the right atrium has the fastest intrinsic rate, about 100 per minute before vagal tone, so it sets the rhythm. The AV node fires at only 40 to 60 per minute and takes over only if the SA node fails, which is why it is called a latent pacemaker.`},{id:`q-ap-cardiovascular-2`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The valve between the left atrium and the left ventricle is the`,options:[`Tricuspid valve`,`Pulmonary valve`,`Mitral valve`,`Aortic valve`],answer:2,explanation:`The mitral or bicuspid valve guards the left atrioventricular opening; the tricuspid guards the right. The aortic and pulmonary valves are semilunar valves at the outflow of the ventricles, not between chamber and chamber.`},{id:`q-ap-cardiovascular-3`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The QRS complex of the electrocardiogram represents`,options:[`Atrial depolarisation`,`Ventricular depolarisation`,`Ventricular repolarisation`,`Atrial repolarisation only`],answer:1,explanation:`The QRS complex is ventricular depolarisation; the T wave is ventricular repolarisation and the P wave atrial depolarisation. Atrial repolarisation does occur but is buried inside the much larger QRS complex, which is why it is never seen as a separate wave.`},{id:`q-ap-cardiovascular-4`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A patient has a heart rate of 75 per minute, end diastolic volume 130 mL and end systolic volume 60 mL. Cardiac output is`,options:[`9.75 L/min`,`3.9 L/min`,`4.5 L/min`,`5.25 L/min`],answer:3,explanation:`Stroke volume = 130 - 60 = 70 mL, so cardiac output = 70 x 75 = 5250 mL/min = 5.25 L/min. Multiplying the end diastolic volume by heart rate gives 9.75 L/min, which is the common error of forgetting that the ventricle never empties completely.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ap-cardiovascular-5`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The plateau phase of the ventricular muscle action potential is mainly due to`,options:[`Closure of all channels`,`Rapid sodium influx`,`Slow calcium influx balancing potassium efflux`,`Chloride influx`],answer:2,explanation:`Phase 2 is maintained by inward current through L-type calcium channels balanced against outward potassium current, giving the long plateau that prevents tetanus of cardiac muscle. Rapid sodium influx is phase 0, the upstroke, and lasts only a couple of milliseconds.`},{id:`q-ap-cardiovascular-6`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The conduction delay of about 0.1 second at the atrioventricular node is physiologically useful because it`,options:[`Gives the atria time to empty into the ventricles before the ventricles contract`,`Slows the heart rate to 72 per minute`,`Prevents coronary blood flow during systole`,`Prevents the ventricle from depolarising twice`],answer:0,explanation:`The AV nodal delay lets atrial systole finish, contributing the final 20 to 30 percent of ventricular filling, before the ventricle contracts. It does not set the overall heart rate, which is determined by the SA node discharge frequency.`},{id:`q-ap-cardiovascular-7`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`If systolic pressure is 120 mm Hg and diastolic pressure is 80 mm Hg, the mean arterial pressure is approximately`,options:[`86 mm Hg`,`110 mm Hg`,`100 mm Hg`,`93 mm Hg`],answer:3,explanation:`MAP = diastolic + one third of pulse pressure = 80 + (40/3) = 93.3 mm Hg. Taking the simple arithmetic mean gives 100 mm Hg, which overestimates because diastole occupies roughly twice as much of the cardiac cycle as systole at normal rates.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ap-cardiovascular-8`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The Frank-Starling law of the heart states that within physiological limits`,options:[`Heart rate is inversely proportional to venous return`,`Force of contraction is proportional to the initial length of the cardiac muscle fibre`,`Cardiac output is independent of preload`,`Contractility rises with increasing afterload`],answer:1,explanation:`Greater end diastolic volume stretches the sarcomeres towards their optimal length and increases calcium sensitivity of the myofilaments, so stroke volume rises with preload. Note this is a change in preload, not contractility - a positive inotrope shifts the whole curve upward, which is a different phenomenon.`},{id:`q-ap-respiratory-1`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The principal muscle of quiet inspiration is the`,options:[`Diaphragm`,`Internal intercostal`,`Rectus abdominis`,`Sternocleidomastoid`],answer:0,explanation:`The diaphragm, supplied by the phrenic nerve from C3, C4 and C5, accounts for about 75 percent of quiet inspiration. The internal intercostals and abdominal muscles are muscles of forced expiration, and sternocleidomastoid is an accessory muscle used only in laboured breathing.`},{id:`q-ap-respiratory-2`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Pulmonary surfactant is secreted by`,options:[`Goblet cells`,`Type I alveolar cells`,`Type II alveolar cells`,`Alveolar macrophages`],answer:2,explanation:`Type II pneumocytes secrete surfactant, chiefly dipalmitoyl phosphatidylcholine, which lowers alveolar surface tension. Type I cells cover about 95 percent of the alveolar surface and are the gas exchange cells but secrete nothing.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ap-respiratory-3`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Given tidal volume 500 mL, inspiratory reserve volume 3000 mL, expiratory reserve volume 1100 mL and residual volume 1200 mL, the vital capacity is`,options:[`3500 mL`,`4600 mL`,`5800 mL`,`2300 mL`],answer:1,explanation:`Vital capacity = IRV + TV + ERV = 3000 + 500 + 1100 = 4600 mL. Adding residual volume as well gives 5800 mL, which is total lung capacity, not vital capacity, since residual volume can never be exhaled.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ap-respiratory-4`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Most carbon dioxide is carried in blood as`,options:[`Carbonic acid in the red cell`,`Dissolved CO2 in plasma`,`Carbaminohaemoglobin`,`Bicarbonate ion`],answer:3,explanation:`About 70 percent of CO2 travels as bicarbonate formed inside the red cell by carbonic anhydrase and exchanged into plasma for chloride, the chloride shift. Carbaminohaemoglobin accounts for roughly 23 percent and dissolved CO2 for only about 7 percent.`},{id:`q-ap-respiratory-5`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A right shift of the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve is produced by`,options:[`Fall in temperature`,`Rise in pH`,`Rise in 2,3-diphosphoglycerate`,`Presence of foetal haemoglobin`],answer:2,explanation:`Raised 2,3-DPG binds deoxyhaemoglobin, lowers oxygen affinity and shifts the curve right so more oxygen is unloaded to tissues, as happens at high altitude and in chronic anaemia. Foetal haemoglobin binds 2,3-DPG poorly and therefore shifts the curve left, which is the opposite direction.`},{id:`q-ap-respiratory-6`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`With a tidal volume of 500 mL, dead space of 150 mL and a respiratory rate of 12 per minute, alveolar ventilation is`,options:[`4.2 L/min`,`1.8 L/min`,`5.0 L/min`,`6.0 L/min`],answer:0,explanation:`Alveolar ventilation = (500 - 150) x 12 = 4200 mL/min = 4.2 L/min. The value 6.0 L/min is pulmonary or minute ventilation, which ignores the anatomical dead space that takes no part in gas exchange.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ap-respiratory-7`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Central chemoreceptors in the medulla respond most directly to`,options:[`Arterial H+ directly crossing the blood-brain barrier`,`Plasma bicarbonate concentration`,`Arterial pO2`,`H+ concentration of cerebrospinal fluid`],answer:3,explanation:`Central chemoreceptors sense the H+ of the cerebrospinal fluid, which changes because CO2 crosses the blood-brain barrier freely and is hydrated to carbonic acid. Arterial H+ itself cannot cross the barrier, which is why a metabolic acidosis stimulates breathing mainly through the peripheral carotid body receptors.`},{id:`q-ap-respiratory-8`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In a patient with a large pulmonary embolus, the affected lung region is best described as having`,options:[`Reduced dead space with increased shunt`,`Increased physiological dead space, V/Q tending to infinity`,`A right to left shunt, V/Q equal to zero`,`Normal V/Q with reduced compliance`],answer:1,explanation:`An embolus stops perfusion while ventilation continues, so the region is ventilated but not perfused and V/Q tends to infinity - alveolar dead space. A V/Q of zero describes a shunt, which is the opposite situation seen in atelectasis or consolidation.`},{id:`q-ap-nervous-1`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Myelination of axons in the central nervous system is carried out by`,options:[`Oligodendrocytes`,`Astrocytes`,`Microglia`,`Schwann cells`],answer:0,explanation:`Oligodendrocytes myelinate CNS axons and one cell can wrap several axons at once. Schwann cells are the tempting distractor but they are peripheral, and each Schwann cell myelinates only one internode of a single axon.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ap-nervous-2`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The part of the brain that controls heart rate, respiration and blood pressure is the`,options:[`Cerebellum`,`Thalamus`,`Medulla oblongata`,`Hypothalamus`],answer:2,explanation:`The medulla oblongata houses the cardiac, vasomotor and respiratory centres, which is why it is called the vital centre. The hypothalamus is the frequent wrong answer; it controls temperature, hunger, thirst and endocrine output rather than beat to beat cardiorespiratory control.`},{id:`q-ap-nervous-3`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The repolarisation phase of the neuronal action potential is caused by`,options:[`Opening of voltage-gated sodium channels`,`Efflux of potassium after sodium channel inactivation`,`Influx of chloride ions`,`Activation of the Na+/K+ ATPase`],answer:1,explanation:`Sodium channels inactivate within about a millisecond while delayed rectifier potassium channels open, so potassium leaving the cell restores the negative interior. The Na+/K+ pump is essential for maintaining gradients over time but is far too slow to produce repolarisation of a single spike.`},{id:`q-ap-nervous-4`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Saltatory conduction occurs because`,options:[`Myelin actively conducts the impulse`,`Potassium channels are absent from myelinated axons`,`The axon diameter increases at intervals`,`Voltage-gated sodium channels are concentrated at the nodes of Ranvier`],answer:3,explanation:`Sodium channels cluster at the nodes while the internodes are insulated by myelin, so current flows passively under the myelin and the impulse regenerates only at each node. Myelin itself is an insulator and conducts nothing; that misreading is the usual trap.`},{id:`q-ap-nervous-5`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The neurotransmitter released at all preganglionic autonomic fibres, both sympathetic and parasympathetic, is`,options:[`Adrenaline`,`Noradrenaline`,`Acetylcholine`,`Dopamine`],answer:2,explanation:`Every preganglionic autonomic fibre is cholinergic and acts on nicotinic receptors in the ganglion. Noradrenaline appears only at postganglionic sympathetic endings, and even there sweat glands are the cholinergic exception.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ap-nervous-6`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The cranial nerve that supplies the lateral rectus muscle of the eye is`,options:[`Abducens (VI)`,`Trigeminal (V)`,`Oculomotor (III)`,`Trochlear (IV)`],answer:0,explanation:`The abducens nerve supplies only the lateral rectus, which abducts the eye, so a lesion causes medial squint. The trochlear supplies the superior oblique and the oculomotor supplies the remaining four extraocular muscles - the rule LR6 SO4, rest 3.`},{id:`q-ap-nervous-7`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In phototransduction, absorption of light by rhodopsin leads to`,options:[`Opening of sodium channels by activated transducin`,`Rise in cGMP through activation of guanylyl cyclase`,`Depolarisation of the rod and increased glutamate release`,`Hyperpolarisation of the rod due to falling cGMP and closure of cation channels`],answer:3,explanation:`Light isomerises 11-cis retinal to all-trans, activating transducin and then phosphodiesterase, so cGMP falls, cGMP-gated cation channels close and the rod hyperpolarises with less glutamate release. Photoreceptors are the classical exception to the rule that a stimulus depolarises a receptor cell.`},{id:`q-ap-nervous-8`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A hemisection of the spinal cord at T10 produces loss of pain and temperature`,options:[`On the same side, below the lesion`,`On the opposite side, below the lesion`,`On both sides equally`,`On the same side, above the lesion`],answer:1,explanation:`Pain and temperature fibres cross within one or two segments of entering the cord and ascend in the contralateral lateral spinothalamic tract, so a hemisection removes them on the opposite side below the lesion. Fine touch and proprioception are lost on the same side because the dorsal columns do not cross until the medulla - the Brown-Sequard pattern.`},{id:`q-ap-digestive-1`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Bile is produced by the liver and stored and concentrated in the`,options:[`Gall bladder`,`Duodenum`,`Spleen`,`Pancreas`],answer:0,explanation:`The gall bladder stores bile between meals and concentrates it 5 to 20 fold by absorbing water and electrolytes. The duodenum is where bile is delivered, not where it is stored, which is the usual mix-up.`},{id:`q-ap-digestive-2`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Hydrochloric acid of the gastric juice is secreted by`,options:[`Mucous neck cells`,`Chief cells`,`Parietal cells`,`G cells`],answer:2,explanation:`Parietal or oxyntic cells secrete both hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor. Chief cells are the standard distractor; they secrete pepsinogen, which the acid from the parietal cells then activates.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ap-digestive-3`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Enterokinase secreted by the duodenal mucosa activates`,options:[`Pepsinogen to pepsin`,`Trypsinogen to trypsin`,`Prolipase to lipase`,`Amylase to maltase`],answer:1,explanation:`Enterokinase, also called enteropeptidase, converts trypsinogen to trypsin, and trypsin then activates chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase and further trypsinogen. Pepsinogen is activated by gastric acid and then autocatalytically, not by enterokinase.`},{id:`q-ap-digestive-4`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Secretin released from the duodenum chiefly causes`,options:[`Contraction of the gall bladder`,`Increased gastric acid secretion`,`Secretion of enzyme-rich pancreatic juice`,`Secretion of bicarbonate-rich pancreatic juice`],answer:3,explanation:`Secretin is released when duodenal pH falls below about 4.5 and acts on pancreatic duct cells to pour out an alkaline bicarbonate-rich juice that neutralises the acid chyme. Cholecystokinin, not secretin, produces the enzyme-rich juice and contracts the gall bladder.`},{id:`q-ap-digestive-5`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Vitamin B12 is absorbed in the`,options:[`Duodenum`,`Jejunum`,`Terminal ileum`,`Colon`],answer:2,explanation:`The B12-intrinsic factor complex has specific receptors in the terminal ileum, which is why ileal resection or Crohn disease causes megaloblastic anaemia. Iron is the substance absorbed in the duodenum, and confusing the two sites is the most common error.`},{id:`q-ap-digestive-6`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The main function of bile salts in fat digestion is`,options:[`Emulsification and micelle formation`,`Activation of pancreatic lipase directly`,`Transport of chylomicrons into lymph`,`Hydrolysis of triglyceride to fatty acids`],answer:0,explanation:`Bile salts are amphipathic detergents that emulsify fat into small droplets and then form mixed micelles that ferry the products of lipolysis to the brush border. Bile contains no enzyme, so it cannot hydrolyse anything; pancreatic lipase with colipase does the actual hydrolysis.`},{id:`q-ap-digestive-7`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Glucose is absorbed from the intestinal lumen into the enterocyte by SGLT1 and leaves across the basolateral membrane by`,options:[`GLUT4`,`PepT1`,`SGLT2`,`GLUT2`],answer:3,explanation:`GLUT2 carries glucose down its gradient out of the enterocyte into portal blood by facilitated diffusion. GLUT4 is the insulin-responsive transporter of muscle and adipose tissue and plays no part in intestinal absorption, and PepT1 carries peptides, not sugars.`},{id:`q-ap-digestive-8`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Long chain fatty acids absorbed from the gut reach the systemic circulation without first passing through the liver because they`,options:[`Are carried in portal blood bound to albumin`,`Are packaged into chylomicrons and enter lymphatic lacteals`,`Are converted to ketone bodies in the enterocyte`,`Diffuse directly across the intestinal capillary endothelium`],answer:1,explanation:`Long chain fatty acids are re-esterified to triglyceride in the enterocyte, packaged with apoB-48 into chylomicrons and enter lacteals, reaching blood by the thoracic duct and so bypassing first pass hepatic metabolism. Short and medium chain fatty acids are the exception - they do enter portal blood, which is why medium chain triglycerides are used in malabsorption.`},{id:`q-ap-renal-endocrine-1`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The structural and functional unit of the kidney is the`,options:[`Nephron`,`Renal pyramid`,`Glomerulus`,`Collecting duct`],answer:0,explanation:`The nephron, comprising the renal corpuscle and its tubule, is the unit that forms urine, and there are about a million in each kidney. The glomerulus is only one part of the nephron, so it cannot be the whole functional unit.`},{id:`q-ap-renal-endocrine-2`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Antidiuretic hormone and oxytocin are synthesised in the`,options:[`Pineal gland`,`Anterior pituitary`,`Hypothalamic supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei`,`Posterior pituitary itself`],answer:2,explanation:`Both hormones are made in hypothalamic neurons and transported down axons to the posterior pituitary, which only stores and releases them. Calling the posterior pituitary their site of synthesis is the standard trap, since it is neural tissue with no secretory cells of its own.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ap-renal-endocrine-3`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Glucose is normally reabsorbed completely in the`,options:[`Collecting duct`,`Proximal convoluted tubule`,`Loop of Henle`,`Distal convoluted tubule`],answer:1,explanation:`All filtered glucose is reclaimed by SGLT2 and SGLT1 in the proximal tubule, so normal urine is glucose free. Glycosuria appears only when plasma glucose exceeds the renal threshold of about 180 mg/dL and the transport maximum is saturated.`},{id:`q-ap-renal-endocrine-4`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Inulin clearance is used to measure glomerular filtration rate because inulin is`,options:[`Filtered and actively secreted`,`Not filtered at all`,`Filtered and completely reabsorbed`,`Filtered, and neither reabsorbed nor secreted`],answer:3,explanation:`Inulin is freely filtered and then handled by no tubular process, so the amount appearing in urine equals the amount filtered and its clearance equals GFR. A substance that is secreted, such as PAH, gives a clearance that measures renal plasma flow instead.`},{id:`q-ap-renal-endocrine-5`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Angiotensin II raises glomerular filtration rate mainly by`,options:[`Increasing the filtration coefficient Kf`,`Dilating the afferent arteriole`,`Constricting the efferent arteriole`,`Constricting the afferent arteriole`],answer:2,explanation:`Angiotensin II preferentially constricts the efferent arteriole, which raises glomerular capillary pressure and preserves filtration when perfusion falls. This is why an ACE inhibitor can precipitate a drop in GFR in a patient dependent on that efferent tone, such as one with bilateral renal artery stenosis.`},{id:`q-ap-renal-endocrine-6`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Excess growth hormone secretion in an adult after epiphyseal closure produces`,options:[`Acromegaly`,`Cretinism`,`Dwarfism`,`Gigantism`],answer:0,explanation:`After the epiphyses fuse the long bones cannot lengthen, so excess growth hormone thickens the hands, feet, jaw and soft tissues - acromegaly. Gigantism is the same hormonal excess occurring before epiphyseal closure, when the bones can still grow in length.`},{id:`q-ap-renal-endocrine-7`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Urine flow is 1 mL/min, urine creatinine 100 mg/dL and plasma creatinine 1 mg/dL. Creatinine clearance is`,options:[`1000 mL/min`,`1 mL/min`,`10 mL/min`,`100 mL/min`],answer:3,explanation:`Clearance = (U x V)/P = (100 x 1)/1 = 100 mL/min, close to a normal GFR. The commonest slip is to forget that the concentration units cancel, leaving an answer in mL/min set only by the flow rate and the concentration ratio.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ap-renal-endocrine-8`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The hyperosmolarity of the renal medulla is generated chiefly by`,options:[`Water reabsorption in the descending limb`,`Active NaCl transport out of the thick ascending limb plus urea recycling`,`Aldosterone action on the collecting duct`,`Rapid blood flow through the vasa recta`],answer:1,explanation:`The water-impermeable thick ascending limb pumps NaCl into the interstitium by NKCC2, and urea recycled from the inner medullary collecting duct contributes about half the 1200 mOsm/kg gradient. Vasa recta flow is deliberately slow and acts as a counter-current exchanger that preserves the gradient rather than creating it.`},{id:`q-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-1`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The total number of bones in the normal adult human skeleton is`,options:[`206`,`226`,`270`,`186`],answer:0,explanation:`An adult has 206 bones - 80 axial and 126 appendicular. The figure 270 is the number present at birth; fusion of several bones, notably the sacrum and coccyx, brings it down to 206.`},{id:`q-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-2`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Cardiac muscle differs from skeletal muscle in that it`,options:[`Contains no sarcoplasmic reticulum`,`Lacks striations`,`Has intercalated discs and is involuntary`,`Is multinucleate with peripheral nuclei`],answer:2,explanation:`Cardiac muscle is striated like skeletal muscle but its cells are joined end to end by intercalated discs carrying gap junctions, and it is involuntary. Lack of striations describes smooth muscle, which is the usual mistaken choice.`},{id:`q-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-3`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`During skeletal muscle contraction, calcium released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum binds to`,options:[`Tropomyosin`,`Troponin C`,`Calmodulin`,`Myosin light chain`],answer:1,explanation:`Ca2+ binds troponin C, which shifts tropomyosin off the myosin binding site on actin so cross-bridges can form. Calmodulin is the calcium receptor of smooth muscle, which contains no troponin at all - that is the distinction being tested.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-4`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`During contraction of a sarcomere, which of the following does NOT shorten?`,options:[`Distance between Z lines`,`I band`,`H zone`,`A band`],answer:3,explanation:`The A band corresponds to the length of the myosin filament, which does not change; only the overlap changes, so the I band, the H zone and the sarcomere length all shorten. This is the direct evidence for the sliding filament theory rather than filament shortening.`},{id:`q-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-5`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Ovulation in a normal 28-day menstrual cycle is triggered by`,options:[`A rise in progesterone on day 10`,`A surge of follicle stimulating hormone on day 5`,`A surge of luteinising hormone at about day 14`,`A fall in oestrogen on day 12`],answer:2,explanation:`Sustained high oestrogen from the mature follicle switches to positive feedback and provokes the LH surge, which ruptures the Graafian follicle about 24 to 36 hours later. Progesterone rises only after ovulation from the corpus luteum, so it is a consequence and not the trigger.`},{id:`q-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-6`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Testosterone is secreted by which cells of the testis, under the control of which hormone?`,options:[`Leydig cells, under LH`,`Spermatogonia, under GnRH`,`Sertoli cells, under LH`,`Sertoli cells, under FSH`],answer:0,explanation:`Interstitial Leydig cells make testosterone in response to LH, while Sertoli cells respond to FSH by nursing germ cells and secreting inhibin and androgen binding protein. Swapping the two cell types and their trophic hormones is the classic error here.`},{id:`q-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-7`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`One primary spermatocyte and one primary oocyte respectively give rise to`,options:[`Two spermatozoa and one ovum`,`One spermatozoon and four ova`,`Four spermatozoa and four ova`,`Four spermatozoa and one ovum with three polar bodies`],answer:3,explanation:`Spermatogenesis divides cytoplasm equally so all four haploid products become spermatozoa, whereas oogenesis divides unequally to conserve cytoplasm in one large ovum, discarding the rest as polar bodies. The unequal cytoplasmic division, not a difference in the number of meiotic divisions, is the reason.`},{id:`q-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-8`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Milk ejection during suckling is mediated by`,options:[`Prolactin acting on alveolar cells`,`Oxytocin contracting myoepithelial cells`,`Oestrogen acting on ductal tissue`,`Human placental lactogen`],answer:1,explanation:`Suckling triggers a neuroendocrine reflex releasing oxytocin, which contracts the myoepithelial cells around the alveoli and squeezes milk into the ducts - the let-down reflex. Prolactin is essential but only for milk synthesis; without oxytocin the milk is made yet cannot be expelled.`},{id:`q-ap-cell-tissue-9`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which organelle carries the electron transport chain on its inner membrane and possesses its own circular DNA?`,options:[`Ribosome`,`Mitochondrion`,`Golgi apparatus`,`Nucleolus`],answer:1,explanation:`The mitochondrion folds its inner membrane into cristae that carry the respiratory chain and ATP synthase, and it holds its own circular DNA inherited only from the mother. The nucleolus is the tempting choice because it also contains nucleic acid, but it only assembles ribosomal RNA and generates no ATP.`},{id:`q-ap-cell-tissue-10`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which cell junction forms a continuous seal that prevents solutes leaking between adjacent epithelial cells?`,options:[`Desmosome`,`Gap junction`,`Tight junction (zonula occludens)`,`Hemidesmosome`],answer:2,explanation:`The tight junction fuses the outer leaflets of neighbouring membranes into a belt that blocks the paracellular route and keeps apical and basolateral membranes chemically different. The gap junction is the opposite arrangement - a connexon pore that deliberately lets ions and small molecules pass from cytoplasm to cytoplasm - while the desmosome and hemidesmosome give only mechanical anchorage.`},{id:`q-ap-cell-tissue-11`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Red blood cells suspended in 0.45 percent sodium chloride solution will`,options:[`Swell and may haemolyse`,`Shrink and become crenated`,`Remain unchanged because the solution is isotonic`,`Lose potassium but keep their original volume`],answer:0,explanation:`Isotonic saline is 0.9 percent, matching a plasma osmolality of about 290 mOsm/kg, so half-normal saline is hypotonic and water moves into the cell until it bursts. Shrinkage with crenation is what a hypertonic solution such as 3 percent saline produces, which is the reverse of what happens here.`},{id:`q-ap-cell-tissue-12`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which option correctly names the chief cation of intracellular fluid and of extracellular fluid, with the usual plasma values?`,options:[`Sodium inside and potassium outside, plasma potassium about 140 mEq/L`,`Calcium inside and sodium outside, plasma calcium about 140 mEq/L`,`Potassium inside and sodium outside, plasma sodium about 135 to 145 mEq/L and plasma potassium about 3.5 to 5 mEq/L`,`Magnesium inside and chloride outside, plasma chloride about 4 mEq/L`],answer:2,explanation:`Potassium is the main intracellular cation at roughly 150 mEq/L inside the cell, while sodium dominates outside; this distribution is created and defended by the Na+/K+ ATPase. Reversing them, so that sodium is intracellular and potassium is the abundant plasma cation, contradicts the direction the pump works in and would abolish the resting potential.`},{id:`q-ap-cell-tissue-13`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The resting membrane potential sits close to the potassium equilibrium potential of about -90 mV rather than the sodium equilibrium potential of about +60 mV because at rest the membrane`,options:[`Is far more permeable to potassium than to sodium`,`Is impermeable to every ion`,`Is more permeable to sodium than to potassium`,`Contains no sodium ions on either side`],answer:0,explanation:`Potassium leak channels make resting permeability to K+ roughly 50 to 100 times that to Na+, so the membrane potential is dragged towards the potassium equilibrium value. The measured -70 mV is a little less negative than -90 mV only because of the small residual sodium leak; if sodium permeability were dominant the cell would sit near +60 mV, which happens only transiently at the peak of an action potential.`},{id:`q-ap-cell-tissue-14`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Transitional epithelium (urothelium) characteristically lines the`,options:[`Trachea and bronchi`,`Oesophagus`,`Small intestine`,`Urinary bladder and ureter`],answer:3,explanation:`Urothelium has surface dome-shaped cells that flatten as the organ distends, which suits the bladder and ureter where volume changes constantly. The trachea is lined by pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium and the oesophagus by stratified squamous epithelium, both of which are built for protection or clearance rather than stretch.`},{id:`q-ap-cell-tissue-15`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A poison that abolishes mitochondrial ATP synthesis will halt which transport process first?`,options:[`Osmosis of water across the membrane`,`Simple diffusion of oxygen`,`Primary active transport by the Na+/K+ ATPase`,`Facilitated diffusion of glucose by a GLUT carrier`],answer:2,explanation:`Only primary active transport hydrolyses ATP at the carrier itself, so the sodium pump stops the moment ATP runs out; secondary active transport then fails as the sodium gradient collapses. Osmosis, simple diffusion of oxygen and GLUT-mediated facilitated diffusion are all passive and need no ATP, so they continue as long as a gradient remains.`},{id:`q-ap-cell-tissue-16`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which feature distinguishes apoptosis from necrosis?`,options:[`Cell swelling with rupture of the plasma membrane`,`Cell shrinkage with an intact membrane and no inflammatory reaction`,`A random smear of DNA on gel electrophoresis`,`Marked inflammation in the surrounding tissue`],answer:1,explanation:`Apoptosis is an energy-requiring, caspase-driven programme in which the cell shrinks, chromatin condenses and membrane-bound apoptotic bodies are phagocytosed, so no contents spill and no inflammation follows; endonuclease cleavage between nucleosomes gives a characteristic DNA ladder. Swelling with membrane rupture, surrounding inflammation and a random DNA smear are all descriptions of necrosis, which is passive and pathological.`},{id:`q-ap-blood-lymph-9`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The normal platelet count in an adult is about`,options:[`1.5 to 4.5 lakh per cubic millimetre`,`4000 to 11000 per cubic millimetre`,`5 million per cubic millimetre`,`20000 to 40000 per cubic millimetre`],answer:0,explanation:`Platelets number roughly 150000 to 450000 per cubic millimetre, that is 1.5 to 4.5 lakh. The figure 4000 to 11000 is the total leucocyte count and 5 million is the red cell count, while 20000 to 40000 is the level below which spontaneous bleeding becomes likely, not a normal value.`},{id:`q-ap-blood-lymph-10`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`In a healthy adult, blood cells are normally produced in the`,options:[`Liver and spleen`,`Thymus`,`Red bone marrow of flat bones and the ends of long bones`,`Lymph nodes`],answer:2,explanation:`After infancy haemopoiesis is confined to red marrow in the sternum, ribs, vertebrae, pelvis, skull and the proximal ends of the femur and humerus. The liver and spleen are foetal sites and resume only as extramedullary haemopoiesis in disease, while the thymus is where T lymphocytes mature rather than where stem cells generate blood cells.`},{id:`q-ap-blood-lymph-11`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The intrinsic pathway of coagulation is set off by`,options:[`Release of tissue factor from injured tissue`,`Conversion of prothrombin to thrombin`,`Binding of calcium to factor VIII`,`Contact of factor XII with a negatively charged surface such as exposed collagen`],answer:3,explanation:`Contact activation of factor XII on collagen or glass starts the intrinsic limb, which then runs through factors XI and IX and takes several minutes, which is why it is the slower pathway. Release of tissue factor is the trigger of the extrinsic limb, which needs only about 15 seconds because it bypasses those contact factors altogether.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ap-blood-lymph-12`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The intrinsic and extrinsic pathways of coagulation converge at the activation of`,options:[`Factor VII`,`Factor X`,`Factor XII`,`Fibrinogen`],answer:1,explanation:`Both limbs end by activating factor X, and Xa with factor Va, calcium and platelet phospholipid forms prothrombinase, which converts prothrombin to thrombin. Fibrinogen is the final substrate at the end of the common pathway rather than its starting point, and factor VII belongs to the extrinsic limb alone.`},{id:`q-ap-blood-lymph-13`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Blockage of lymphatic drainage, as in filariasis, produces oedema mainly because`,options:[`Capillary hydrostatic pressure falls`,`Plasma albumin is lost in the urine`,`Protein that leaks into the interstitium is no longer removed, so interstitial oncotic pressure rises`,`Venous pressure falls below interstitial pressure`],answer:2,explanation:`Lymphatics return about 2 to 4 litres of fluid a day together with the plasma protein that escapes from capillaries; when they are blocked that protein accumulates and holds water in the tissue, giving a high-protein oedema. Loss of albumin in the urine also causes oedema, but by lowering plasma oncotic pressure - a different Starling term and a different disease, nephrotic syndrome.`},{id:`q-ap-blood-lymph-14`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Plasmin, the enzyme that digests fibrin, is generated from plasminogen by`,options:[`Antithrombin III`,`Alpha-2 antiplasmin`,`Thrombin acting on factor XIII`,`Tissue plasminogen activator`],answer:3,explanation:`Tissue plasminogen activator from endothelium, along with urokinase and the drug streptokinase, converts plasminogen to plasmin, which cleaves fibrin into fibrin degradation products including D-dimer. Antithrombin III and alpha-2 antiplasmin are inhibitors, not activators - the first neutralises thrombin and factor Xa and is the target of heparin, the second quenches free plasmin.`},{id:`q-ap-blood-lymph-15`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which statement correctly contrasts heparin with warfarin?`,options:[`Heparin acts by activating antithrombin III and works both in vitro and in vivo, while warfarin blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase and works only in vivo`,`Both act only in vivo and both take two to three days to produce an effect`,`Heparin blocks vitamin K dependent carboxylation while warfarin activates antithrombin III`,`Warfarin acts immediately and is reversed by protamine sulphate`],answer:0,explanation:`Heparin binds antithrombin III and accelerates inhibition of thrombin and factor Xa at once, so it anticoagulates a sample of blood in a test tube as well as a patient, and it is reversed by protamine sulphate. Warfarin acts on hepatic synthesis of factors II, VII, IX and X, so nothing happens until the already circulating factors decay over two to three days, it has no effect in vitro, and it is reversed by vitamin K or fresh frozen plasma.`},{id:`q-ap-blood-lymph-16`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A man has a measured plasma volume of 3 litres and a haematocrit of 40 percent. His total blood volume is`,options:[`7.5 litres`,`4.2 litres`,`5 litres`,`3.4 litres`],answer:2,explanation:`Plasma makes up (100 - 40) = 60 percent of blood, so blood volume = 3 / 0.6 = 5 litres, of which 2 litres is cell volume. Dividing the plasma volume by the haematocrit itself, giving 7.5 litres, is the usual slip - the haematocrit is the cell fraction, not the plasma fraction.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ap-cardiovascular-9`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The ejection fraction of a normal left ventricle at rest is about`,options:[`25 to 35 percent`,`55 to 70 percent`,`90 to 95 percent`,`100 percent`],answer:1,explanation:`Stroke volume of about 70 mL out of an end diastolic volume of about 130 mL gives an ejection fraction of roughly 55 to 70 percent. A value near 100 percent is impossible because the ventricle never empties completely, and 25 to 35 percent is the range seen in systolic heart failure.`},{id:`q-ap-cardiovascular-10`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The first heart sound is produced by`,options:[`Closure of the aortic and pulmonary valves`,`Opening of the atrioventricular valves`,`Rapid passive filling of the ventricle`,`Closure of the mitral and tricuspid valves at the onset of ventricular systole`],answer:3,explanation:`The first sound is the low-pitched, longer lub of atrioventricular valve closure and marks the beginning of ventricular systole. Closure of the aortic and pulmonary valves gives the shorter, sharper second sound at the start of diastole, and rapid ventricular filling produces the third heart sound, which is normal only in children and young adults.`},{id:`q-ap-cardiovascular-11`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A ventricle has an end diastolic volume of 150 mL and an end systolic volume of 60 mL. Its ejection fraction is`,options:[`40 percent`,`90 percent`,`60 percent`,`150 percent`],answer:2,explanation:`Stroke volume = 150 - 60 = 90 mL, so ejection fraction = 90/150 x 100 = 60 percent. The value 40 percent is the residual fraction, that is end systolic volume divided by end diastolic volume, and 90 percent comes from quoting the stroke volume in millilitres as if it were a percentage.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ap-cardiovascular-12`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The correct order in which an impulse travels through the conducting system of the heart is`,options:[`SA node, AV node, internodal tracts, Purkinje fibres, bundle of His`,`SA node, internodal atrial pathways, AV node, bundle of His, bundle branches, Purkinje fibres`,`AV node, SA node, bundle of His, bundle branches, Purkinje fibres`,`SA node, bundle of His, AV node, bundle branches, Purkinje fibres`],answer:1,explanation:`The impulse starts in the SA node, spreads over the atria through internodal pathways to the AV node, is delayed there, and then runs down the bundle of His, its branches and the Purkinje network to the ventricular myocardium. Placing the bundle of His before the AV node inverts the anatomy, since the His bundle is the continuation of the AV node and the only muscular bridge across the fibrous skeleton.`},{id:`q-ap-cardiovascular-13`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Immediately after a moderate haemorrhage, the baroreceptor reflex produces`,options:[`Increased baroreceptor firing with reflex bradycardia`,`Vasodilatation of skin and splanchnic vessels`,`Withdrawal of sympathetic tone to the heart`,`Reduced baroreceptor firing, tachycardia and widespread vasoconstriction`],answer:3,explanation:`A fall in pressure stretches the carotid sinus and aortic arch less, so their afferent firing drops, the vasomotor centre is released from inhibition, sympathetic outflow rises and vagal tone falls - giving tachycardia, increased contractility and vasoconstriction of skin and splanchnic beds. Increased firing with bradycardia is the mirror-image response to a rise in blood pressure, not to blood loss.`},{id:`q-ap-cardiovascular-14`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Vagal stimulation slows the heart because acetylcholine acting on M2 receptors of the SA node`,options:[`Increases potassium conductance and flattens the slope of phase 4 depolarisation`,`Increases the funny current If by raising cyclic AMP`,`Blocks L-type calcium channels of ventricular muscle`,`Lengthens the plateau of the ventricular action potential`],answer:0,explanation:`M2 receptors act through Gi to lower cyclic AMP and to open potassium channels, so the pacemaker cell hyperpolarises and its phase 4 slope flattens, taking longer to reach threshold; conduction through the AV node also slows. Raising cyclic AMP and increasing the funny current is exactly what sympathetic beta 1 stimulation does, producing the opposite effect on rate.`},{id:`q-ap-cardiovascular-15`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In the cardiac cycle, the phase that immediately follows closure of the aortic valve is`,options:[`Ventricular ejection`,`Atrial systole`,`Isovolumetric relaxation, with all four valves shut`,`Isovolumetric contraction`],answer:2,explanation:`Aortic closure ends ejection, and ventricular pressure then falls steeply at constant volume with every valve closed until it drops below atrial pressure and the mitral valve opens for filling. Isovolumetric contraction is the other all-valves-closed phase, but it follows closure of the atrioventricular valves at the start of systole, so the two are easily interchanged.`},{id:`q-ap-cardiovascular-16`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Oxygen consumption is 250 mL/min, arterial oxygen content 20 mL per 100 mL of blood and mixed venous oxygen content 15 mL per 100 mL. By the Fick principle, cardiac output is`,options:[`1.25 L/min`,`50 L/min`,`5 L/min`,`0.5 L/min`],answer:2,explanation:`The arteriovenous oxygen difference is 5 mL per 100 mL, that is 0.05 mL per mL of blood, so cardiac output = 250 / 0.05 = 5000 mL/min = 5 L/min. Dividing by the arterial content alone gives 1.25 L/min, and quoting 250 divided by 5 as litres gives 50 L/min - both come from mishandling the units rather than the physiology.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ap-respiratory-9`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`In a healthy adult breathing room air, arterial oxygen and carbon dioxide tensions are about`,options:[`pO2 about 40 mm Hg and pCO2 about 46 mm Hg`,`pO2 about 160 mm Hg and pCO2 about 0.3 mm Hg`,`pO2 about 60 mm Hg and pCO2 about 60 mm Hg`,`pO2 about 95 to 100 mm Hg and pCO2 about 40 mm Hg`],answer:3,explanation:`Arterial blood normally carries an oxygen tension of about 95 to 100 mm Hg and a carbon dioxide tension of about 40 mm Hg, with saturation around 97 percent. The pair 40 and 46 mm Hg are the mixed venous values, and 160 mm Hg with almost no carbon dioxide describes dry atmospheric air before humidification and gas exchange.`},{id:`q-ap-respiratory-10`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`At the end of a quiet expiration, the intrapleural pressure is about`,options:[`Minus 5 cm H2O, that is below atmospheric pressure`,`Plus 5 cm H2O, above atmospheric pressure`,`Equal to atmospheric pressure`,`Minus 30 cm H2O`],answer:0,explanation:`The inward elastic recoil of the lung and the outward pull of the chest wall keep the pleural space subatmospheric, about minus 5 cm H2O at the end of expiration and about minus 8 cm H2O at the height of inspiration. Pleural pressure becomes equal to atmospheric only when the space is opened, as in pneumothorax, and the lung then collapses.`},{id:`q-ap-respiratory-11`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Tidal volume is 500 mL, inspiratory reserve volume 3000 mL, expiratory reserve volume 1100 mL and residual volume 1200 mL. The functional residual capacity and the inspiratory capacity are respectively`,options:[`1600 mL and 4600 mL`,`3500 mL and 2300 mL`,`2300 mL and 3500 mL`,`4600 mL and 5800 mL`],answer:2,explanation:`FRC = ERV + RV = 1100 + 1200 = 2300 mL, and IC = TV + IRV = 500 + 3000 = 3500 mL. Interchanging the two values is the standard error; 4600 mL is the vital capacity and 5800 mL the total lung capacity, neither of which is being asked for here.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ap-respiratory-12`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The Bohr effect describes`,options:[`Deoxygenated haemoglobin carrying more carbon dioxide`,`A rise in carbon dioxide and H+ lowering the oxygen affinity of haemoglobin`,`The movement of chloride into the red cell as bicarbonate leaves`,`The greater oxygen affinity of foetal haemoglobin`],answer:1,explanation:`The Bohr effect is the right shift of the oxygen dissociation curve caused by the carbon dioxide and acid produced in active tissue, so oxygen is unloaded exactly where it is needed. Deoxygenated haemoglobin carrying more carbon dioxide is the reciprocal Haldane effect, and the chloride shift is a separate anion exchange that keeps the red cell electrically neutral.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ap-respiratory-13`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which of the following cannot be measured by simple spirometry?`,options:[`Vital capacity`,`Functional residual capacity`,`Tidal volume`,`Inspiratory capacity`],answer:1,explanation:`Any value that contains residual volume cannot be measured by spirometry because residual volume never leaves the lung, so FRC, RV and total lung capacity need helium dilution, nitrogen washout or body plethysmography. Vital capacity, tidal volume and inspiratory capacity are all made up of air that can actually be moved in or out of the mouthpiece.`},{id:`q-ap-respiratory-14`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`During an attack of bronchial asthma the FEV1/FVC ratio is`,options:[`Normal or even raised, which is the restrictive pattern`,`Always exactly 100 percent`,`Unchanged, because FEV1 and FVC fall to the same extent`,`Reduced below about 70 percent, which is the obstructive pattern`],answer:3,explanation:`A normal ratio is about 80 percent; in airway obstruction the forced expiratory volume in one second falls much more than the forced vital capacity, so the ratio drops. In a restrictive disease such as pulmonary fibrosis both values fall together, leaving the ratio normal or even higher than normal, which is the distinction being tested.`},{id:`q-ap-respiratory-15`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Alveolar hypoxia in one region of the lung makes the pulmonary arterioles supplying that region`,options:[`Dilate, exactly as systemic arterioles do in hypoxia`,`Constrict, diverting blood to better ventilated alveoli`,`Stay unaffected, since pulmonary vessels have no smooth muscle`,`Constrict only if the arterial carbon dioxide tension also falls`],answer:1,explanation:`The pulmonary circulation is the great exception to the rule that hypoxia dilates blood vessels: hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction shunts blood away from poorly ventilated alveoli and so protects ventilation-perfusion matching. When hypoxia is generalised, as at altitude or in chronic obstructive lung disease, the same reflex raises pulmonary artery pressure and eventually causes cor pulmonale.`},{id:`q-ap-respiratory-16`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Haemoglobin is 15 g/dL and arterial saturation 98 percent. Taking the oxygen capacity of haemoglobin as 1.34 mL per gram, the oxygen carried bound to haemoglobin in 100 mL of arterial blood is about`,options:[`About 3 mL`,`About 0.3 mL`,`About 200 mL`,`About 20 mL`],answer:3,explanation:`Bound oxygen = 15 x 1.34 x 0.98 = about 19.7 mL per 100 mL of blood, which is why arterial oxygen content is quoted as roughly 20 volumes percent. About 0.3 mL is the physically dissolved oxygen in the same 100 mL, and that tiny figure is the reason a severely anaemic patient cannot be sustained on dissolved gas alone.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ap-nervous-9`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Taste from the anterior two thirds of the tongue is carried by`,options:[`The glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)`,`The hypoglossal nerve (XII)`,`The facial nerve (VII), through the chorda tympani`,`The trigeminal nerve (V)`],answer:2,explanation:`The chorda tympani branch of the facial nerve carries taste from the front two thirds of the tongue, which is why a facial nerve lesion above that branch causes loss of taste as well as facial weakness. The glossopharyngeal nerve serves the posterior one third, the hypoglossal nerve is purely motor to the tongue muscles, and the lingual branch of the trigeminal carries ordinary touch from the anterior tongue but not taste.`},{id:`q-ap-nervous-10`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A lesion of the cerebellum characteristically produces`,options:[`Ataxia, intention tremor and loss of coordination`,`Resting tremor with rigidity and bradykinesia`,`Loss of pain and temperature on the opposite side of the body`,`Complete paralysis of all voluntary movement`],answer:0,explanation:`The cerebellum smooths and coordinates movement rather than initiating it, so its lesions give ataxia, dysmetria, nystagmus, dysdiadochokinesia and a tremor that worsens as the finger approaches the target - all on the same side as the lesion. Resting tremor with rigidity and bradykinesia is the basal ganglia picture of Parkinson disease, and confusing a resting tremor with an intention tremor is the usual error.`},{id:`q-ap-nervous-11`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A patient follows spoken commands perfectly but speaks only in short, effortful, telegraphic phrases. The lesion is most likely in`,options:[`The Wernicke area of the superior temporal gyrus`,`The angular gyrus`,`The cerebellar vermis`,`The Broca area of the inferior frontal gyrus of the dominant hemisphere`],answer:3,explanation:`Damage to the Broca area gives a motor or expressive aphasia: comprehension is preserved, output is sparse and laboured, and the patient is painfully aware of the deficit. A Wernicke area lesion gives the mirror picture - fluent, well-articulated but meaningless speech with poor comprehension and no insight.`},{id:`q-ap-nervous-12`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Sympathetic and parasympathetic stimulation of the iris respectively produce`,options:[`Miosis through the dilator pupillae, and mydriasis through the constrictor pupillae`,`Mydriasis by contraction of the radial dilator pupillae, and miosis by contraction of the circular constrictor pupillae`,`Mydriasis by both, differing only in the speed of response`,`No change in pupil size, since only light intensity alters it`],answer:1,explanation:`Sympathetic alpha 1 receptors contract the radially arranged dilator pupillae and widen the pupil, while parasympathetic fibres running with the oculomotor nerve reach M3 receptors on the circular sphincter and narrow it, also producing accommodation. Attributing dilatation to the circular muscle reverses the anatomy - it is the radial arrangement of the fibres that determines which way the pupil moves.`},{id:`q-ap-nervous-13`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`During the absolute refractory period no second action potential can be produced however strong the stimulus, because`,options:[`The sodium-potassium pump is temporarily switched off`,`All potassium channels are closed`,`Voltage-gated sodium channels are in the inactivated state`,`The membrane is hyperpolarised below the potassium equilibrium potential`],answer:2,explanation:`The inactivation gates of the sodium channels are shut and can only reset once the membrane has repolarised, so no regenerative inward current is possible; this sets the upper limit on firing frequency and stops the impulse travelling backwards. In the relative refractory period, by contrast, some channels have recovered and a larger than normal stimulus can fire the cell - that is the distinction being tested.`},{id:`q-ap-nervous-14`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which statement about cerebrospinal fluid is correct?`,options:[`It is secreted by the choroid plexus and absorbed into the dural venous sinuses through arachnoid villi, total volume about 150 mL`,`It is secreted by the arachnoid villi and absorbed by the choroid plexus, total volume about 500 mL`,`It is formed in the subarachnoid space alone and totals about 1500 mL`,`It is a simple ultrafiltrate identical in composition to plasma`],answer:0,explanation:`The choroid plexuses of the ventricles secrete about 500 mL a day, but only about 150 mL is present at any moment, so the fluid turns over three to four times daily before draining through arachnoid villi into the venous sinuses. It is not a plain filtrate: compared with plasma it has much less protein and glucose, less potassium and calcium and slightly more chloride, because it is actively secreted.`},{id:`q-ap-nervous-15`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Bilateral destruction of the ventromedial nucleus of the hypothalamus produces`,options:[`Loss of appetite with severe wasting`,`Diabetes insipidus with polyuria`,`Hyperphagia and obesity`,`Loss of temperature regulation alone`],answer:2,explanation:`The ventromedial nucleus is the satiety centre, so removing it takes the brake off feeding and the animal overeats and becomes obese. The lateral hypothalamic area is the feeding or hunger centre and its destruction causes aphagia and wasting, while polyuria from failure of ADH follows damage to the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei or their tract.`},{id:`q-ap-nervous-16`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which nerve fibres conduct most slowly, and are among the first whose function is lost when a local anaesthetic is given?`,options:[`A alpha fibres supplying skeletal muscle`,`A beta fibres carrying touch and pressure`,`A gamma fibres supplying muscle spindles`,`Unmyelinated C fibres carrying slow pain and postganglionic sympathetic traffic`],answer:3,explanation:`C fibres are unmyelinated and only about 0.5 to 2 micrometres across, so they conduct at roughly 0.5 to 2 m/s, and clinically pain and autonomic function are lost before touch and motor power. Note a subtlety worth carrying into the exam: on intrinsic susceptibility the small myelinated B fibres are usually described as the most readily blocked of all, because myelination lets the anaesthetic act at only a few nodes of Ranvier - so a question asking strictly which fibre type is most susceptible may key B rather than C. The large myelinated A alpha fibres conduct fastest at about 70 to 120 m/s and are the last to succumb to local anaesthetic, even though they are the first to fail under pressure or hypoxia.`},{id:`q-ap-digestive-9`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Digestion of starch begins in the mouth through the action of`,options:[`Pepsin`,`Lingual lipase`,`Salivary amylase (ptyalin)`,`Maltase`],answer:2,explanation:`Ptyalin works best at a pH of about 6.8 and breaks starch down to dextrins and maltose, stopping once it meets gastric acid. Lingual lipase is genuinely present in saliva but acts on fat, and pepsin is a gastric enzyme that acts only on protein, so neither begins carbohydrate digestion.`},{id:`q-ap-digestive-10`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Gastrin is secreted by`,options:[`Chief cells of the fundus`,`G cells of the gastric antrum`,`Enterochromaffin-like cells`,`S cells of the duodenum`],answer:1,explanation:`G cells of the antrum release gastrin in response to peptides and amino acids in the lumen, distension of the stomach and vagal gastrin releasing peptide. Enterochromaffin-like cells are the tempting choice because they sit in the same wall, but they supply the histamine through which gastrin drives the parietal cell; S cells of the duodenum secrete secretin instead.`},{id:`q-ap-digestive-11`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The phases of gastric secretion, in the order in which they occur, are`,options:[`Gastric, then cephalic, then intestinal`,`Intestinal, then gastric, then cephalic`,`Cephalic, then intestinal, then gastric`,`Cephalic, then gastric, then intestinal, contributing roughly 20 to 30 percent, 60 to 70 percent and 10 percent of the response`],answer:3,explanation:`Sight, smell, taste and even the thought of food start the cephalic phase entirely through the vagus; food entering the stomach then drives the largest, gastric phase through distension and gastrin; and chyme in the duodenum adds a small intestinal contribution that quickly turns inhibitory through the enterogastric reflex, secretin and GIP. Putting the gastric phase first ignores that secretion begins before any food has been swallowed, which is exactly what vagotomy abolishes.`},{id:`q-ap-digestive-12`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide (GIP) is released in response to`,options:[`Glucose and fat in the duodenum, and it stimulates insulin release from pancreatic beta cells`,`A fall in duodenal pH below 4.5, and it stimulates bicarbonate secretion`,`Distension of the stomach, and it contracts the gall bladder`,`Fasting, and it starts the migrating motor complex`],answer:0,explanation:`GIP is an incretin: nutrients arriving in the duodenum make it prime the beta cell before plasma glucose has risen much, which is why an oral glucose load releases more insulin than the identical load given intravenously. A fall in duodenal pH is the stimulus for secretin, and contraction of the gall bladder is the action of cholecystokinin, so both of those describe different hormones.`},{id:`q-ap-digestive-13`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The migrating motor complex that sweeps the empty gut between meals is triggered by`,options:[`Cholecystokinin released after a fatty meal`,`Gastrin released by antral distension`,`Motilin released in the fasting state`,`Vasoactive intestinal peptide released during eating`],answer:2,explanation:`Motilin from duodenal M cells rises in bursts every 90 to 120 minutes while fasting and drives the housekeeper wave that clears residue and bacteria from stomach to ileum; erythromycin is a motilin agonist, which is why it is used as a prokinetic. Cholecystokinin and gastrin are fed-state hormones, and eating actually interrupts the complex rather than starting it.`},{id:`q-ap-digestive-14`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Unconjugated bilirubin differs from conjugated bilirubin in that it`,options:[`Is water soluble and appears in the urine`,`Gives a direct van den Bergh reaction`,`Is formed in the intestine by bacterial action`,`Is water insoluble, circulates bound to albumin and does not appear in the urine`],answer:3,explanation:`Unconjugated bilirubin is lipid soluble and travels attached to albumin, so it cannot be filtered at the glomerulus - haemolytic jaundice therefore gives no bilirubin in the urine, but the free pigment can cross the immature blood-brain barrier and cause kernicterus. Conjugation with glucuronic acid inside the hepatocyte makes it water soluble, so only that form gives a direct van den Bergh reaction and spills into urine in obstructive jaundice.`},{id:`q-ap-digestive-15`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A gastrin-secreting tumour causes steatorrhoea as well as peptic ulceration because the excess acid`,options:[`Destroys bile salts within the gall bladder`,`Lowers duodenal pH so far that pancreatic lipase is inactivated and bile acids are precipitated`,`Prevents the release of cholecystokinin`,`Blocks the uptake of fat by the lacteals`],answer:1,explanation:`Pancreatic lipase needs a near-neutral pH, so an acid load that swamps duodenal bicarbonate denatures the enzyme and precipitates bile acids, leaving fat neither hydrolysed nor carried in micelles. The ulcers arise directly from the same acid load and are typically multiple, recurrent and sometimes beyond the duodenal bulb, which is the clue to the diagnosis.`},{id:`q-ap-digestive-16`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Of the bile salts delivered to the intestine each day, the proportion returned to the liver by the enterohepatic circulation is about`,options:[`About 20 percent`,`None, since all bile salts are excreted after a single pass`,`About 95 percent, with only about 5 percent lost in the faeces`,`About 50 percent, the remainder being synthesised afresh each day`],answer:2,explanation:`Roughly 95 percent of bile salts are actively reclaimed in the terminal ileum and carried back in portal blood, so a pool of only about 3 to 4 g recycles six to eight times a day and the liver need only replace the small faecal loss. This explains why an anion exchange resin that traps bile salts in the gut, or resection of the ileum, forces the liver to consume cholesterol to make more.`},{id:`q-ap-renal-endocrine-9`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Insulin is secreted by which cells of the islets of Langerhans?`,options:[`Alpha cells`,`Delta cells`,`PP cells`,`Beta cells`],answer:3,explanation:`Beta cells form roughly 60 to 70 percent of the islet, lie in its centre and secrete insulin along with C peptide. Alpha cells secrete glucagon and delta cells secrete somatostatin, which restrains both of the others - so naming the alpha cell is the usual slip.`},{id:`q-ap-renal-endocrine-10`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Erythropoietin is secreted mainly by`,options:[`The peritubular interstitial cells of the kidney in response to hypoxia`,`The liver in response to hypoxia`,`The red bone marrow`,`The adrenal cortex`],answer:0,explanation:`About 90 percent of erythropoietin comes from interstitial cells around the renal tubules, which is why chronic kidney disease produces a normocytic normochromic anaemia that responds to recombinant erythropoietin. The liver supplies the remaining fraction and is the main source in the foetus, while the bone marrow is the target of the hormone rather than its source.`},{id:`q-ap-renal-endocrine-11`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Glomerular filtration rate is 125 mL/min and renal plasma flow is 625 mL/min. The filtration fraction is`,options:[`0.05, that is 5 percent`,`0.2, that is 20 percent`,`5, that is 500 percent`,`0.65, that is 65 percent`],answer:1,explanation:`Filtration fraction = GFR / renal plasma flow = 125/625 = 0.2, so about a fifth of the plasma reaching the glomerulus is filtered. Inverting the ratio to give 5 is the common arithmetic slip, and using renal blood flow of about 1200 mL/min instead of plasma flow gives roughly 0.1 - the denominator must be plasma, because red cells are not filtered.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ap-renal-endocrine-12`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which statement correctly separates the action of antidiuretic hormone from that of aldosterone?`,options:[`ADH reabsorbs sodium while aldosterone reabsorbs water`,`Both act mainly on the proximal convoluted tubule`,`ADH inserts aquaporin-2 channels in the collecting duct and reabsorbs water alone, while aldosterone reabsorbs sodium in exchange for potassium and H+`,`ADH comes from the adrenal cortex and aldosterone from the posterior pituitary`],answer:2,explanation:`ADH acts on V2 receptors to move aquaporin-2 into the apical membrane of the collecting duct, so water is retained without solute and the urine is concentrated; excess therefore dilutes the plasma and causes hyponatraemia. Aldosterone acts on the principal cells to retain sodium isotonically with water while potassium and H+ are lost, so its excess raises blood pressure with hypokalaemia and alkalosis but leaves plasma sodium nearly normal.`},{id:`q-ap-renal-endocrine-13`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Glucagon raises blood glucose almost entirely by acting on`,options:[`Skeletal muscle, releasing glucose from muscle glycogen`,`Adipose tissue, by recruiting GLUT4 transporters`,`The brain, which depends on it for glucose uptake`,`The liver, stimulating glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis`],answer:3,explanation:`Glucagon raises cyclic AMP in the hepatocyte, driving glycogen breakdown, gluconeogenesis and, in starvation, ketogenesis. Skeletal muscle is the tempting answer because it stores the most glycogen, but it has essentially no glucagon receptors and, lacking glucose-6-phosphatase, could not release free glucose into blood even if stimulated; recruitment of GLUT4 is an insulin action, and brain uptake is insulin-independent.`},{id:`q-ap-renal-endocrine-14`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Parathyroid hormone raises plasma calcium by`,options:[`Increasing calcium and phosphate reabsorption together in the proximal tubule`,`Stimulating osteoclastic bone resorption, increasing distal tubular calcium reabsorption and activating vitamin D in the kidney`,`Inhibiting osteoclasts and increasing urinary calcium loss`,`Acting directly on the intestinal mucosa to absorb calcium`],answer:1,explanation:`PTH works by three routes - resorption of bone, reclaiming calcium in the distal tubule, and 1-alpha-hydroxylation of 25-hydroxycholecalciferol to calcitriol, which is what actually increases absorption from the gut, since PTH itself has no direct intestinal action. It also blocks phosphate reabsorption in the proximal tubule, so plasma calcium (normally about 9 to 11 mg/dL) and phosphate move in opposite directions.`},{id:`q-ap-renal-endocrine-15`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Plasma sodium is 140 mEq/L, chloride 104 mEq/L and bicarbonate 16 mEq/L. The anion gap is`,options:[`10 mEq/L, which is normal`,`36 mEq/L`,`20 mEq/L, which is raised`,`120 mEq/L`],answer:2,explanation:`Anion gap = Na+ - (Cl- + HCO3-) = 140 - (104 + 16) = 20 mEq/L, above the usual teaching range of about 8 to 16 mEq/L, so unmeasured anions have accumulated - diabetic ketoacidosis, lactic acidosis, uraemia or poisoning by salicylate or methanol. A low bicarbonate with a normal gap points instead to diarrhoea or renal tubular acidosis, where chloride rises to take the place of the lost bicarbonate.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ap-renal-endocrine-16`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which set of findings fits primary adrenocortical insufficiency (Addison disease)?`,options:[`Hypertension with hypokalaemia and metabolic alkalosis`,`Hypertension with truncal obesity and hyperglycaemia`,`Hypocalcaemia with carpopedal spasm`,`Hypotension with hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia and hyperpigmentation`],answer:3,explanation:`Loss of aldosterone means sodium and water are lost while potassium and H+ are retained, giving hypotension, hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia and a mild acidosis; loss of cortisol removes feedback on the pituitary, so ACTH rises and darkens the skin and buccal mucosa. Hypertension with hypokalaemic alkalosis is the mirror image, primary hyperaldosteronism, and truncal obesity with hyperglycaemia and hypertension is cortisol excess in Cushing syndrome.`},{id:`q-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-9`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The elbow is an example of which type of synovial joint?`,options:[`Ball and socket`,`Pivot`,`Hinge`,`Saddle`],answer:2,explanation:`A hinge joint is uniaxial and allows only flexion and extension, which is exactly what the elbow, knee and ankle do. The ball and socket arrangement of the shoulder and hip permits movement in three planes, the pivot joint of the atlas and axis permits rotation, and the saddle joint at the base of the thumb permits opposition.`},{id:`q-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-10`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The hormone detected by a urine pregnancy test is`,options:[`Progesterone from the corpus luteum`,`Human chorionic gonadotropin from the syncytiotrophoblast`,`Luteinising hormone from the anterior pituitary`,`Human placental lactogen`],answer:1,explanation:`Human chorionic gonadotropin appears in blood and urine within days of implantation and rescues the corpus luteum so progesterone does not fall and menstruation does not occur. Progesterone rises in every luteal phase whether or not conception has taken place, so it cannot be diagnostic; and because hCG closely resembles luteinising hormone, test kits must use an antibody directed at its beta subunit.`},{id:`q-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-11`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which statement is true of smooth muscle but not of skeletal muscle?`,options:[`It has well developed T tubules at the A-I junction`,`It is multinucleate with nuclei placed at the periphery`,`It contains no troponin, uses calmodulin as its calcium receptor and draws much of its calcium from outside the cell`,`It fatigues rapidly during sustained contraction`],answer:2,explanation:`Smooth muscle has a single central nucleus, no troponin at all, and works through calcium-calmodulin activating myosin light chain kinase, with caveolae taking the place of T tubules and much of the calcium entering from the extracellular fluid. Multinucleate fibres with peripheral nuclei, T tubules at the A-I junction and rapid fatigue all describe skeletal muscle, and smooth muscle is in fact remarkably fatigue-resistant because of the latch state.`},{id:`q-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-12`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Neostigmine reverses a block produced by d-tubocurarine but deepens a block produced by suxamethonium because`,options:[`d-Tubocurarine is a competitive antagonist that accumulated acetylcholine can displace, whereas suxamethonium blocks by keeping the end plate depolarised`,`d-Tubocurarine acts presynaptically while suxamethonium acts postsynaptically`,`Neostigmine destroys d-tubocurarine chemically`,`Suxamethonium prevents acetylcholine release from the nerve terminal`],answer:0,explanation:`Inhibiting acetylcholinesterase piles up acetylcholine at the junction, which outcompetes a reversible competitive blocker but only sustains the depolarisation that a depolarising agent has already produced. Preventing acetylcholine release from the terminal is the mechanism of botulinum toxin, not of suxamethonium, which binds the same nicotinic receptor as acetylcholine and activates it persistently.`},{id:`q-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-13`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A woman has a regular 35-day menstrual cycle. Ovulation is expected at about`,options:[`Day 14`,`Day 17 or 18`,`Day 21`,`Day 28`],answer:2,explanation:`The luteal phase is fixed at about 14 days because the corpus luteum survives only that long without hCG, so ovulation falls 35 - 14 = about day 21; it is the follicular phase that stretches or shortens as cycle length varies. Assuming day 14 for every woman is right only for a 28-day cycle and is the classic error in fertility timing.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-14`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Osteoclasts are best described as`,options:[`Mesenchymal cells that lay down osteoid and secrete alkaline phosphatase`,`Multinucleate cells of the monocyte-macrophage lineage that resorb bone and are inhibited by calcitonin`,`Cells inhibited directly by parathyroid hormone`,`Mature cells trapped in lacunae that maintain the matrix`],answer:1,explanation:`Osteoclasts descend from the haemopoietic monocyte-macrophage line and dissolve mineral and matrix through a ruffled border that secretes acid and cathepsin K; calcitonin inhibits them directly, which is how it lowers plasma calcium. Laying down osteoid and secreting alkaline phosphatase is the job of the osteoblast, and parathyroid hormone stimulates resorption indirectly through RANKL on osteoblasts rather than inhibiting it.`},{id:`q-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-15`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Excitation-contraction coupling in skeletal muscle differs from that in cardiac muscle in that skeletal muscle`,options:[`Requires an influx of extracellular calcium for every contraction`,`Has no sarcoplasmic reticulum`,`Uses calmodulin rather than troponin C as its calcium receptor`,`Uses a mechanical link between the dihydropyridine receptor and the ryanodine receptor, so it can go on contracting briefly in a calcium-free medium`],answer:3,explanation:`In skeletal muscle the voltage-sensing dihydropyridine receptor of the T tubule is physically coupled to the ryanodine receptor of the sarcoplasmic reticulum, so depolarisation opens the internal store directly and contraction survives for a time without extracellular calcium. Cardiac muscle instead needs a trigger influx through its L-type calcium channels to open its ryanodine receptors - calcium-induced calcium release - which is exactly why calcium channel blockers weaken the heart yet leave skeletal muscle power intact.`},{id:`q-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-16`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Testosterone is converted by 5-alpha-reductase and by aromatase respectively into`,options:[`Oestradiol, which drives prostatic growth, and dihydrotestosterone, which closes the epiphyses`,`Dihydrotestosterone, which drives prostatic growth and male pattern baldness, and oestradiol, which closes the epiphyses`,`Androstenedione and dehydroepiandrosterone`,`Cortisol and aldosterone`],answer:1,explanation:`Dihydrotestosterone is the more potent androgen at the prostate and the hair follicle, which is why finasteride, a 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor, is used in benign prostatic hyperplasia and androgenetic alopecia. Epiphyseal closure in both sexes is mediated by oestradiol made by aromatase, so a man who lacks aromatase keeps growing - assigning prostatic growth to oestradiol and epiphyseal closure to dihydrotestosterone reverses both roles.`}],n=[{id:`f-ap-cell-tissue-1`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,front:`Which organelle detoxifies drugs and synthesises steroids?`,back:`Smooth endoplasmic reticulum.`},{id:`f-ap-cell-tissue-2`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,front:`Name the four basic tissue types.`,back:`Epithelial, connective, muscular, nervous.`},{id:`f-ap-cell-tissue-3`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,front:`Stoichiometry of the sodium-potassium pump?`,back:`3 Na+ out, 2 K+ in, per ATP hydrolysed.`},{id:`f-ap-cell-tissue-4`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,front:`Who proposed the fluid mosaic model, and when?`,back:`Singer and Nicolson, 1972.`},{id:`f-ap-cell-tissue-5`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,front:`Normal blood pH range?`,back:`7.35 to 7.45.`},{id:`f-ap-cell-tissue-6`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,front:`Epithelium of the alveoli and of blood capillaries?`,back:`Simple squamous epithelium.`},{id:`f-ap-blood-lymph-1`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,front:`Lifespan of an erythrocyte and of a platelet?`,back:`RBC about 120 days; platelet 7 to 10 days.`},{id:`f-ap-blood-lymph-2`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,front:`Vitamin K dependent clotting factors?`,back:`II, VII, IX, X plus proteins C and S.`},{id:`f-ap-blood-lymph-3`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,front:`Universal donor and universal recipient blood groups?`,back:`O negative donor; AB positive recipient.`},{id:`f-ap-blood-lymph-4`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,front:`Which test monitors warfarin and which monitors heparin?`,back:`Prothrombin time (INR) for warfarin; aPTT for unfractionated heparin.`},{id:`f-ap-blood-lymph-5`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,front:`Formula for MCHC?`,back:`MCHC = (Hb x 100) / PCV, expressed in g/dL; normal 32 to 36.`},{id:`f-ap-blood-lymph-6`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,front:`Normal total leucocyte count?`,back:`4000 to 11000 per cubic millimetre.`},{id:`f-ap-cardiovascular-1`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,front:`Intrinsic rates of SA node, AV node and Purkinje fibres?`,back:`SA about 100/min, AV 40 to 60/min, Purkinje 20 to 40/min.`},{id:`f-ap-cardiovascular-2`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,front:`Formula for cardiac output?`,back:`CO = stroke volume x heart rate; about 5 L/min at rest.`},{id:`f-ap-cardiovascular-3`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,front:`Formula for mean arterial pressure?`,back:`MAP = diastolic + one third of pulse pressure.`},{id:`f-ap-cardiovascular-4`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,front:`What causes the plateau of the ventricular action potential?`,back:`Slow inward L-type calcium current balancing potassium efflux.`},{id:`f-ap-cardiovascular-5`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,front:`What does the ECG P wave and T wave represent?`,back:`P is atrial depolarisation; T is ventricular repolarisation.`},{id:`f-ap-cardiovascular-6`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,front:`State the Frank-Starling law in one line.`,back:`Force of contraction is proportional to initial fibre length, so stroke volume rises with preload.`},{id:`f-ap-respiratory-1`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,front:`Which cell makes surfactant and what is its main component?`,back:`Type II pneumocyte; dipalmitoyl phosphatidylcholine.`},{id:`f-ap-respiratory-2`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,front:`Formula for vital capacity?`,back:`VC = IRV + TV + ERV; about 4600 mL. Add RV to get TLC.`},{id:`f-ap-respiratory-3`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,front:`Formula for alveolar ventilation?`,back:`VA = (tidal volume - dead space) x respiratory rate; about 4.2 L/min.`},{id:`f-ap-respiratory-4`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,front:`In what forms is CO2 carried in blood?`,back:`Bicarbonate about 70 percent, carbamino about 23 percent, dissolved about 7 percent.`},{id:`f-ap-respiratory-5`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,front:`Name four causes of a right shift of the O2 dissociation curve.`,back:`Raised CO2, raised H+ (low pH), raised temperature, raised 2,3-DPG.`},{id:`f-ap-respiratory-6`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,front:`Laplace law for an alveolus?`,back:`P = 2T / r; surfactant lowers T more in small alveoli and prevents collapse.`},{id:`f-ap-nervous-1`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,front:`Resting membrane potential and threshold of a neuron?`,back:`About -70 mV at rest; threshold about -55 mV.`},{id:`f-ap-nervous-2`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,front:`Which ion causes depolarisation and which causes repolarisation?`,back:`Na+ influx depolarises; K+ efflux repolarises.`},{id:`f-ap-nervous-3`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,front:`Chief excitatory and chief inhibitory transmitters of the brain?`,back:`Glutamate excitatory; GABA inhibitory (glycine in the spinal cord).`},{id:`f-ap-nervous-4`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,front:`Which cranial nerve supplies the lateral rectus and which the superior oblique?`,back:`Abducens (VI) lateral rectus; trochlear (IV) superior oblique. LR6 SO4, rest 3.`},{id:`f-ap-nervous-5`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,front:`Sympathetic and parasympathetic outflow levels?`,back:`Sympathetic thoracolumbar T1-L2; parasympathetic craniosacral III, VII, IX, X and S2-S4.`},{id:`f-ap-nervous-6`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,front:`Which sensation reaches the cortex without relaying in the thalamus?`,back:`Smell (olfaction).`},{id:`f-ap-digestive-1`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,front:`Which enzyme does bile contain?`,back:`None. Bile emulsifies fat with bile salts but has no digestive enzyme.`},{id:`f-ap-digestive-2`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,front:`What do parietal cells secrete?`,back:`Hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor.`},{id:`f-ap-digestive-3`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,front:`Stimulus and action of secretin?`,back:`Duodenal acid below pH 4.5; causes bicarbonate-rich pancreatic juice.`},{id:`f-ap-digestive-4`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,front:`What activates trypsinogen?`,back:`Enterokinase (enteropeptidase) from the duodenal mucosa; trypsin then autoactivates.`},{id:`f-ap-digestive-5`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,front:`Where are iron, vitamin B12 and bile salts absorbed?`,back:`Iron in the duodenum; B12 and bile salts in the terminal ileum.`},{id:`f-ap-digestive-6`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,front:`Which clotting factor is not made by the liver?`,back:`Factor VIII, made largely by vascular endothelium.`},{id:`f-ap-renal-endocrine-1`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,front:`Normal GFR and renal blood flow?`,back:`GFR about 125 mL/min (180 L/day); RBF about 1200 mL/min, 20 to 25 percent of cardiac output.`},{id:`f-ap-renal-endocrine-2`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,front:`Clearance formula?`,back:`Cx = (Ux x V) / Px, in mL/min. Inulin clearance = GFR; PAH clearance = effective renal plasma flow.`},{id:`f-ap-renal-endocrine-3`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,front:`Renal threshold for glucose?`,back:`About 180 mg/dL of plasma; transport maximum about 375 mg/min.`},{id:`f-ap-renal-endocrine-4`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,front:`Sequence of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system?`,back:`Renin, angiotensinogen to angiotensin I, ACE in lung, angiotensin II, aldosterone from zona glomerulosa.`},{id:`f-ap-renal-endocrine-5`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,front:`Deficiency and excess of ADH?`,back:`Deficiency gives diabetes insipidus; excess gives SIADH with dilutional hyponatraemia.`},{id:`f-ap-renal-endocrine-6`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,front:`What do the three zones of the adrenal cortex secrete?`,back:`Glomerulosa aldosterone, fasciculata cortisol, reticularis androgens - salt, sugar, sex.`},{id:`f-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-1`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,front:`Number of bones in the adult skeleton, axial and appendicular?`,back:`206 total: 80 axial, 126 appendicular.`},{id:`f-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-2`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,front:`Which band of the sarcomere does not shorten during contraction?`,back:`The A band; the I band and H zone shorten.`},{id:`f-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-3`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,front:`Calcium receptor in skeletal versus smooth muscle?`,back:`Troponin C in skeletal and cardiac; calmodulin in smooth muscle.`},{id:`f-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-4`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,front:`Why does rigor mortis occur?`,back:`ATP is exhausted, so myosin heads cannot detach from actin.`},{id:`f-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-5`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,front:`Which hormones do Sertoli and Leydig cells respond to?`,back:`Sertoli cells to FSH (secrete inhibin); Leydig cells to LH (secrete testosterone).`},{id:`f-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-6`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,front:`Which hormone makes milk and which ejects it?`,back:`Prolactin synthesises milk; oxytocin ejects it by contracting myoepithelial cells.`},{id:`f-ap-cell-tissue-7`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,front:`Chief cation inside and outside the cell, with plasma values?`,back:`K+ inside (about 150 mEq/L intracellular); Na+ outside, plasma Na+ about 135 to 145 and plasma K+ about 3.5 to 5 mEq/L.`},{id:`f-ap-cell-tissue-8`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,front:`Tight junction versus gap junction versus desmosome?`,back:`Tight junction seals the paracellular route; gap junction lets ions pass cell to cell; desmosome only anchors mechanically.`},{id:`f-ap-cell-tissue-9`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,front:`Apoptosis versus necrosis in one line?`,back:`Apoptosis: shrinkage, intact membrane, DNA ladder, no inflammation. Necrosis: swelling, rupture, DNA smear, inflammation.`},{id:`f-ap-cell-tissue-10`,topicId:`ap-cell-tissue`,front:`Plasma osmolality and the isotonic saline concentration?`,back:`About 290 mOsm/kg; isotonic saline is 0.9 percent NaCl.`},{id:`f-ap-blood-lymph-7`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,front:`Normal platelet count and normal haemoglobin?`,back:`Platelets 1.5 to 4.5 lakh per cubic mm; Hb about 13 to 17 g/dL in men, 12 to 15 g/dL in women.`},{id:`f-ap-blood-lymph-8`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,front:`What triggers the intrinsic and the extrinsic pathway?`,back:`Intrinsic: factor XII contacting collagen or a charged surface. Extrinsic: tissue factor from injured tissue.`},{id:`f-ap-blood-lymph-9`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,front:`Where does the common pathway begin and end?`,back:`Begins with factor X activation; ends with fibrin cross-linked by factor XIII.`},{id:`f-ap-blood-lymph-10`,topicId:`ap-blood-lymph`,front:`Mechanism and antidote of heparin versus warfarin?`,back:`Heparin activates antithrombin III, immediate, works in vitro too, reversed by protamine. Warfarin blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase, takes 2 to 3 days, in vivo only, reversed by vitamin K.`},{id:`f-ap-cardiovascular-7`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,front:`Ejection fraction formula and normal value?`,back:`EF = stroke volume / end diastolic volume x 100; normally about 55 to 70 percent.`},{id:`f-ap-cardiovascular-8`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,front:`Order of the conducting system?`,back:`SA node, internodal atrial pathways, AV node, bundle of His, bundle branches, Purkinje fibres.`},{id:`f-ap-cardiovascular-9`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,front:`What produces the first and second heart sounds?`,back:`S1 closure of mitral and tricuspid valves at the start of systole; S2 closure of aortic and pulmonary valves at the start of diastole.`},{id:`f-ap-cardiovascular-10`,topicId:`ap-cardiovascular`,front:`Fick principle for cardiac output?`,back:`CO = O2 consumption / (arterial - venous O2 content); 250 mL/min over 5 mL per 100 mL gives 5 L/min.`},{id:`f-ap-respiratory-7`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,front:`Formulas for inspiratory capacity and functional residual capacity?`,back:`IC = TV + IRV, about 3500 mL; FRC = ERV + RV, about 2300 mL.`},{id:`f-ap-respiratory-8`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,front:`Normal arterial pO2 and pCO2, and the venous values?`,back:`Arterial about 95 to 100 and about 40 mm Hg; mixed venous about 40 and about 46 mm Hg.`},{id:`f-ap-respiratory-9`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,front:`Bohr effect versus Haldane effect?`,back:`Bohr: CO2 and H+ lower the O2 affinity of Hb. Haldane: deoxygenated Hb carries more CO2.`},{id:`f-ap-respiratory-10`,topicId:`ap-respiratory`,front:`FEV1/FVC in obstructive versus restrictive disease?`,back:`Normal about 80 percent. Obstructive: reduced, below about 70 percent. Restrictive: normal or raised, with both volumes low.`},{id:`f-ap-nervous-7`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,front:`Broca versus Wernicke aphasia?`,back:`Broca (inferior frontal): non-fluent, effortful speech, comprehension intact. Wernicke (superior temporal): fluent but meaningless speech, comprehension lost.`},{id:`f-ap-nervous-8`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,front:`CSF: where formed, where absorbed, how much?`,back:`Formed by choroid plexus, about 500 mL a day; about 150 mL present at a time; absorbed by arachnoid villi into dural venous sinuses.`},{id:`f-ap-nervous-9`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,front:`Which nerves carry taste from the tongue?`,back:`Anterior two thirds by the chorda tympani of the facial nerve (VII); posterior one third by the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX).`},{id:`f-ap-nervous-10`,topicId:`ap-nervous`,front:`Effect of destroying the ventromedial versus the lateral hypothalamus?`,back:`Ventromedial (satiety centre) destroyed gives hyperphagia and obesity; lateral (feeding centre) destroyed gives aphagia and wasting.`},{id:`f-ap-digestive-7`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,front:`Three phases of gastric secretion and their share?`,back:`Cephalic about 20 to 30 percent (vagal), gastric about 60 to 70 percent (distension and gastrin), intestinal about 10 percent.`},{id:`f-ap-digestive-8`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,front:`Stimulus and actions of cholecystokinin?`,back:`Fatty acids and amino acids in the duodenum; contracts the gall bladder, relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, gives enzyme-rich pancreatic juice.`},{id:`f-ap-digestive-9`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,front:`What is an incretin?`,back:`A gut hormone (GIP, GLP-1) released by nutrients in the duodenum that stimulates insulin release, so oral glucose raises insulin more than intravenous glucose.`},{id:`f-ap-digestive-10`,topicId:`ap-digestive`,front:`How much of the bile salt pool is recycled?`,back:`About 95 percent reabsorbed in the terminal ileum; the 3 to 4 g pool recycles 6 to 8 times a day.`},{id:`f-ap-renal-endocrine-7`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,front:`Filtration fraction formula and normal value?`,back:`FF = GFR / renal plasma flow = 125/625 = about 0.2, that is 20 percent.`},{id:`f-ap-renal-endocrine-8`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,front:`Anion gap formula and normal range?`,back:`AG = Na+ - (Cl- + HCO3-); normally about 8 to 16 mEq/L. Raised gap means added acid such as ketones or lactate.`},{id:`f-ap-renal-endocrine-9`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,front:`ADH versus aldosterone in the collecting duct?`,back:`ADH inserts aquaporin-2 and reabsorbs water alone; aldosterone reabsorbs Na+ with water while excreting K+ and H+.`},{id:`f-ap-renal-endocrine-10`,topicId:`ap-renal-endocrine`,front:`Electrolytes in Addison disease versus Conn syndrome?`,back:`Addison: low Na+, high K+, acidosis, hypotension, pigmentation. Conn: high Na+ tendency, low K+, alkalosis, hypertension.`},{id:`f-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-7`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,front:`How do you find the day of ovulation in a cycle of any length?`,back:`Cycle length minus 14, because the luteal phase is fixed at about 14 days. A 35-day cycle ovulates about day 21.`},{id:`f-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-8`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,front:`How does skeletal muscle differ from cardiac muscle in E-C coupling?`,back:`Skeletal: dihydropyridine receptor mechanically opens the ryanodine receptor, no extracellular Ca2+ needed. Cardiac: calcium-induced calcium release needs trigger Ca2+ from outside.`},{id:`f-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-9`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,front:`Why does neostigmine reverse curare but not suxamethonium?`,back:`Curare is a competitive blocker that extra acetylcholine displaces; suxamethonium blocks by persistent depolarisation, which more acetylcholine only sustains.`},{id:`f-ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive-10`,topicId:`ap-musculoskeletal-reproductive`,front:`Products of 5-alpha-reductase and of aromatase acting on testosterone?`,back:`Dihydrotestosterone (prostate, male pattern baldness) and oestradiol (epiphyseal closure in both sexes).`}];export{n as flashcards,e as notes,t as questions};